    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Cafe-Associate_JR101494": {
      "title": "Cafe Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : Entry level position on the café team responsible for assisting in front-and back-of-house responsibilities: prepares and cooks high quality food; staffs any function in the front-of-house, as needed; maintains food quality, sanitation, and safety standards; and provides exemplary customer service. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: This position is designated as essential personnel and, during inclement weather and emergency situations, is required to be available to the University as needed. This position requires long periods of standing and walking, and the ability to lift 50 lbs., in conditions that are often, hot, humid, or cold. Must be able to safely use and operate typical kitchen tools and equipment. Must be able to work outdoors in varying weather conditions, on occasion. Youth age 16 and 17 are not permitted to operate, feed, set-up, adjust, repair or clean hazardous equipment, e.g., chopping or slicing machines, commercial mixers, power driven bakery machines, trash compactor, etc. QUALIFICATIONS : Basic knowledge of culinary techniques and practices Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices Basic knowledge of computers and the ability to learn software Ability to efficiently and accurately operate point-of-sale register systems Ability to use effective time management to accomplish tasks Skilled in reading and written and verbal communication Ability to understand and follow written and verbal instructions and procedures Ability to exercise flexibility and to work calmly and efficiently under pressure in a fast-paced environment with frequent interruptions Ability to provide a high level of customer service Ability to monitor and maintain quality control standards Ability to work cooperatively as a part of a team EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE : High school diploma or GED required or in the process of pursing a high school diploma or GED which must be obtained within 2 years of date of hire Minimum of 1 year cooking/ cold food prep and cashiering experience preferred Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire (This is dependent on the assigned unit.) WORK HOURS :"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Catering-Cook-II_JR101464-1": {
      "title": "Catering Cook II",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY :  Primary food production staff in University Catering requiring a high level of cooking and food presentation. Works under direction and exercises some decision making. Prepares food for catering and commissary customers and is responsible for maintaining high standards for food quality while practicing proper sanitation, food safety, and cooking procedures. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Food service QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to simultaneously handle multiple priorities Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills Possesses flexibility to work in a fast paced, dynamic environment Friendly and service-oriented Demonstrates a high commitment to quality Excellent collaboration and team building skills Intermediate knowledge of culinary techniques and practices Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices Skilled in high-volume and high-quality food production and presentation EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE :  H.S. Diploma or GED required 2 years of experience in high volume production in hotel, restaurant, or college food service required Safe food handler certification or certification obtained within one year of hire. (Requirement dependent on assigned unit.) WORK HOURS : Full Time, Non - Exempt Sunday - Saturday, hours vary, weekends needed SALARY: Pay Grade 8 - ($17.75/hour to $19.25/hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Manager-Accounting_JR101480": {
      "title": "Manager Accounting",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Description ***** For consideration, please attach a resume and cover letter***** SUMMARY : The Accounting Manager will be responsible for the day-to-day operations of the Accounting office and maintains direct oversight and responsibility for the University’s investment and endowment accounting. Additionally, this position is a resource to the campus community regarding accounting matters, performs and manages the process to prepare and complete the 990/990T/4720 tax preparations, and supports the annual audit of the University’s consolidated financial statements. This position will also assist with the design and implementation of continued process modernization and improvements. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Management/Leadership Responsibilities Directly supervise Accounting Analyst(s). Manage the day-to-day activities of the Accounting Office. Mentor and develop accounting office staff. Assume responsibility for the Accounting Office in the absence of the Director. General and Endowment Accounting Responsibilities Perform complex accounting for the University’s charitable gift annuities, outside trusts, operating investments, and other investments. Perform endowment accounting at the University including determining endowment market value, understanding donor restrictions, and completing various monthly/quarterly/yearly reports or surveys. Review accounting entries and reconciliations prepared by other team members of the Accounting office. Perform complex monthly reconciliations involving multiple systems and deep understanding of underlying accounting entries and campus processes. Ensure reconciliation of all general ledger activity on a monthly or quarterly basis, as appropriate. Prepare schedules and disclosures needed for the University’s quarterly and annual financial statements in accordance with the Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB) guidance, including Accounting Standards Codification (ASC) Topic 820, Fair Value Measurements and Disclosures. Coordinate with Spider Management Company (SMC) on new investments.   Analysis and Reporting Create, with the Director, the quarterly and annual financial statements and other external reports. Lead preparation of the University’s annual 990 and related tax filings, working with external tax accountants to ensure correct and timely reporting. Perform/resolve all state tax correspondence/inquiries in cooperation with the director and SVP/Controller of the University. Analyze finances for Board reporting, senior leadership reporting, and departmental reporting. Assist the Director in the coordination and preparation of annual external financial statement audits. System Administration, Education, Training and Compliance Mentor and assists campus on endowment and general accounting policies. Evaluate and assess current practices and procedures, make recommendations for improvement Assist with special projects as necessary. Assist University departments in various accounting/finance issues. Other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Supervises staff, including hiring, scheduling, and assigning work, providing input in performance reviews, promotions, or terminations. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Successfully works in an open, collaborative office environment Ability to sit for long periods of time Ability to walk to points on campus within a one-mile radius Ability to lift – up to 10 pounds. QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability: Thorough understanding of US GAAP, specifically FASB based accounting, including extensive knowledge of accounting guidance related to investments and not-for-profit accounting Excellent verbal and written communication skills across all levels of organization including external contacts Strong time management and organizational skills Knowledge of employee relationship building and performance management techniques Advanced Microsoft Excel with proficiency in other Microsoft products, specifically Word, Access and PowerPoint Preferred experience with common higher education ERPs (Banner, Workday, Oracle, etc.). Ability to create queries and reports from databases EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE : Bachelor’s degree in accounting or finance 5-7 year’s experience in higher education, public accounting, or not-for-profit accounting CPA or CPA candidate preferred WORK HOURS : Full Time, Exempt Monday - Friday 7.75 hours/daily, 38.75 hours/weekly SALARY STRUCTURE : Pay Grade 8 (Hiring Range $68,474.00 to $101,858.00 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Deputy-Director-of-the-Bonner-Center-for-Civic-Engagement--CCE-_JR101414": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of the Bonner Center for Civic Engagement (CCE)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Description Applications submitted by September 14th will be given priority review. A cover letter is required  to be considered for this position. POSITION SUMMARY: The Deputy Director of the Bonner Center for Civic Engagement (CCE) plays a critical role in connecting academic study and cross-campus programming at the University with community engagement in order to strengthen communities and support students in leading lives of purpose. The Deputy Director leads strategic coordination, alignment, and collaboration across the CCE’s stakeholder teams to further UR’s mission. The Deputy Director promotes cohesiveness and collaboration amongst CCE programs, guiding program evolution in alignment with the Center’s mission, vision, values and community-driven action plan . The Deputy Director will also lead the Center’s strategy for assessing the effectiveness of programs and initiatives and for assessing University-wide community engagement. The Deputy Director role will lead within the CCE’s values-driven culture in order to ensure that the CCE provides the highest quality support for community-engaged faculty and students across the University and for community partners.    JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Leadership, Management, and Staff Culture (55%) Manages the CCE’s human resources, including: provide direct supervision for 3 Sr Associate Directors, each overseeing stakeholder-facing teams with responsibility for multiple programs across curricular and co-curricular domains; provide direct supervision for 1 Assocate Director of Community-Engaged Scholarship. Convenes and facilitates regular meetings and communication streams across programs and teams to align, coordinate, and resolve program challenges and to ensure staff culture reflects CCE values, including all-staff meetings, trainings, and retreats. Engages and promotes cohesiveness and alignment amongst CCE programs and campus and community partnerships that touch multiple university and external programs Leads and oversees the design, coordination, and implementation of ongoing, field-based training and development programs for CCE staff and offers learning opportunities to other University staff with community engagement responsibilities. Supports CCE’s culture of distributed and facilitative leadership by supporting working groups and other cross-CCE teams in reaching group goals and fostering transparent and inclusive decision-making. Cultivates and aligns collaborations with campus leaders and with faculty in order to support University goals related to community engagement. Serves as a representative of the Bonner Center with both internal and external audiences while exercising considerable judgment in articulating an ambitious vision, viewpoints and agendas on behalf of the Bonner Center. Serves as a thought leader; contributes to visibility of the Bonner Center at national meetings, and brings insights and new developments from the field to inform program development and vice versa. Represents the CCE and the University on relevant institutional and civic committees and working groups. Strategy and Operations (25%) Provides guidance to the Executive Director of the CCE by helping shape the strategy for collaboration across the CCE and peer offices, including the establishment of short-term and long-range shared goals, objectives, policies, and operating procedures that align with a values-driven culture; prioritizes areas of improvement, aligning project scopes with available resources, and encouraging effective change management practices. Leads program teams in aligning and operationalizing specific annual plans that advance the CCE’s and University’s strategic priorities. Develops and implements strategic and tactical plans for the improvement of CCE programs and activities, in alignment with CCE’s community-driven action plan. Develops and launches strategic initiatives and special projects; incubates and determines ways to integrate into CCE structure if appropriate. Leads development of practices and policies for programs; supports strategic resource development and allocation. Shapes strategy, annual planning, use of internal resources, and performance of community-engaged transportation; this includes co-leadership (with Transportation and Parking) of Transportation Working Group to coordinate and advocate for community-engaged transportation needs across the University. Coordinates year-end reporting from program and stakeholder teams. Program Administration and Assessment (20%)  Leads Center-wide Assessment team to drive Center’s assessment strategy and support on-going program improvement and learning, in collaboration with Associate Director, Operations. Leads and oversees the development and implementation of program assessment frameworks. Sets the strategy and oversees the implementation of effective data collection and management systems in order to evaluate the effectiveness of the University’s community engagement and outreach efforts.  Oversees implementation of best practices in design, facilitation, and evaluation of academically grounded community engagement initiatives, including community-based and community-engaged learning classes and community-engaged scholarship, student engagement and civic learning, and community partnerships. CONTACTS: CCE leadership team and Executive Director - to develop and guide strategy and assessment CCE Staff – to support collaboration and professional development Faculty members, Associate Deans, and Deans across schools—to promote and support community-based learning and civic engagement across the curriculum  Student Life Deans—to strengthen connections and educational ties to co-curricular programs  Directors—to collaborate across units, including Global Engagement, will, Inclusion and Belonging, Student Hub for Inclusion and Belonging, Athletics, Modlin Center, and HR—to coordinate and integrate community engagement efforts across campus in conjunction with the University’s strategic plan  Students—to advise, support and promote community engagement as part of their curricular and co-curricular learning  Community, government leaders, non-profit leaders—to forge and strengthen partnerships, to promote the mission of the CCE, and to meet community-identified needs  Institutional Effectiveness staff and community partners—to work on assessment of UR’s community efforts, UR’s community impact, and the metro Richmond community’s needs and assets  Transportation—to coordinate and advocate for community-engaged transportation needs SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Sr. Associate Director, Community-Engaged Learning Sr. Associate Director, Student Engagement Sr. Associate Director, Community Relationships Associate Director, Community-Engaged Research, Scholarship, and Relationships QUALIFICATIONS: Strong oral and written communication skills and ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences  Ability to lead, motivate and supervise staff with a focus on professional development  Knowledge of higher education administration and of faculty career pathways Knowledge and experience in assessment  Knowledge of the role of civic education in higher education  Knowledge of non-profits  Managerial experience  Effective collaboration with multiple constituencies on campus and in the local and academic communities  Collaborative leadership skills, including ability to communicate effectively in varied settings  Ability to build strong relationships with faculty, students and staff as well as leaders of community organizations  Organizational and operational skills to plan and oversee programming, assessments, and planning  Teaching/educating experience  Research experience in field related to the CCE’s mission  Ability to critically review and implement policies and procedures  Ability to articulate mission and core activities of the CCE and UR to broad constituents, on campus and the wider local and academic communities  Skills and experience in facilitation and convening groups who might not normally come together  Academic and/or practical understanding of contemporary civic and social issues in national and local contexts  Working knowledge of experiential education, community-based research and service learning  Collaborative work style  Exceptional judgment, strong initiative, poise, and patience EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: MA required; Ph.D. preferred 8-10 years' experience in higher education and demonstrated experience working with students, faculty, administrators and community partners with preference given to work in civic education. WORK SCHEDULE: Full-time; Exempt Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM SALARY STRUCTURE: Grade 9 ($77,854 to $107,058 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Classical-Studies_JR101487": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Classical Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Classical Studies in the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. We are seeking a scholar with an active program of research in ancient medicine and a strong commitment to undergraduate teaching and mentorship. Areas of expertise in which we are particularly interested include, but are not limited to: Hellenistic Egypt, religious ritual and magic, gender and sexuality, race and ethnic identity, disability studies, reception of Hippocratic Corpus and Galen, and papyrology. Research programs involving digital humanities are especially welcome. In addition to a course focused on the history of ancient medicine (CLSC 210), the successful candidate will teach Greek and Latin language courses and mentor undergraduate student research. The candidate will be able to develop new courses that enhance the department’s existing curricular offerings and can further support our General Education curriculum, the Web of Inquiry , including opportunities to contribute to the University’s interdisciplinary programs. The regular teaching load will be five courses per academic year across two semesters. The Department of Classical Studies strives to sustain an engaging and collaborative environment that welcomes students from all backgrounds into the study of the languages, material cultures, histories, and receptions of the ancient Mediterranean world, with special emphasis on ancient Greek and Latin. The successful candidate will broaden the range of scholarly specialties, viewpoints, and experiences represented within the department.  We are interested in applicants who have a commitment to creating inclusive learning environments, mentoring students from a range of backgrounds and experiences, and generating student interest in Classics. A Ph.D. in Classics or a related field is required by the time of appointment. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit as separate .pdf files: a curriculum vitae, cover letter (two pages maximum), teaching portfolio, research statement, and a sample or excerpt of published or unpublished scholarship (15-25 pages). The teaching portfolio should be a single.pdf file including a one-page statement of teaching philosophy, list of courses taught and the applicant’s role in each course, teaching evaluations from at least one course (if available), and a sample syllabus for a Greek or Latin course (intermediate or upper level). The research statement (two pages maximum) should describe the applicant’s current research, future research plans, and potential contributions to undergraduate research and the intellectual life of the University of Richmond. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence October 14, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Elizabeth Baughan ( ebaughan@richmond.edu ) with any questions. For more information on the Classical Studies Department and its programs, please visit classics.richmond.edu. The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $85,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/HR-Coordinator_JR101486-1": {
      "title": "HR Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: The Human Resources (HR) Coordinator is the first point of contact for Human Resources, creating a welcoming, professional experience for employees, students, applicants, visitors, and the general public. This position is responsible for delivering outstanding customer service, resolving routine customer inquiries and concerns, and ensuring timely and accurate responses to a wide variety of HR-related requests. The HR Coordinator proactively identifies customer needs, researches solutions, answers questions, and resolves issues while escalating complex matters to the appropriate HR team member. In addition to front-line customer support, this role performs administrative and operational duties that support the daily functions of the Human Resources department, including transaction processing, records management, scheduling, financial support, and special projects. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Front Desk Reception & Service - 50% · Develops working knowledge of HR policies, practices, and established compliance requirements to provide accurate information and support routine HR processes · Serves as the primary point of contact for the Human Resources department, greeting employees, students, applicants, visitors, and vendors in a professional, courteous, and welcoming manner. · Responds to a high volume of in-person, telephone, and email inquiries regarding HR services, policies, benefits, payroll, compensation, talent management, employment opportunities, and general University information. · Delivers exceptional customer service through active listening, identifying employee needs, and providing accurate, timely, and effective information or assistance. · Resolves routine customer questions and concerns independently through research, sound judgment, and knowledge of HR processes and policies while appropriately escalating complex issues. · Ensures adequate follow-up with employees, department representatives, and managers as necessary to ensure resolution of problems involving incomplete, inaccurate, or non-compliant documentation. · Maintains a welcoming, organized, and customer-focused reception area that reflects professionalism and operational excellence. · Receives, reviews, and processes documents pertaining to compliance needs for employees, including I-9 forms; audits information for completeness and accuracy with established policy and procedural requirements. Administrative & HR Operations Support – 30% · Manages Administrative Floater requests/assignments by matching assignments with the most qualified available floater. · Maintains office supply inventory; coordinates supply orders for HR staff; organizes and maintains an orderly supply/break room. · Coordinates office equipment, maintenance, and compliance needs. · Assists HR department with administrative support such as document preparation, mailings, and data entry support. · Assists with scanning, filing, mail distribution, scheduling, data cleanup, and other administrative support activities. · Maintains office supplies and department purchasing. · Helps maintain organized workspaces and shared office areas to promote an efficient customer experience. Financial & Administrative support – 10% · Allocates credit card expenses to the appropriate account and reconciles procurement card statements on a monthly basis. · Allocates expenses and pays invoices as requested by HR staff. · Monitors, tracks, and reconciles actual departmental expenses as compared to budgeted expenses on a monthly basis · Maintains appropriate documentation to support departmental expenses. · Enters and maintains departmental contracts in the Contract Management System. Other Duties – 10 % · Provides backup and assistance to HR Representatives on a rotational basis or as otherwise required. · Schedules meetings, interviews, and department events as needed. · Assists various HR areas with assignments, special projects, or events as assigned. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: In person 8:30am – 5:00pm Monday - Friday Office environment No or very limited exposure to physical risk No or very limited physical effort required QUALIFICATIONS: Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service and creating a positive employee experience. Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to build positive relationships with employees, students, and colleagues. Strong verbal and written communication skills with an emphasis on open, collaborative, and professional communication. Demonstrated commitment to employee excellence through responsiveness, professionalism, respect, and accountability. Ability to resolve customer concerns calmly, professionally, and efficiently while exercising sound judgment. Close attention to detail with the ability to maintain accuracy while managing multiple priorities. Strong organizational and time management skills with the ability to meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Ability to quickly assess situations, gather relevant information, and identify appropriate solutions. Ability to work collaboratively as a member of a team while also working independently. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive information with discretion. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and standard office technology. Experience using HRIS, payroll, or other business information systems preferred. Strong data entry, records management, and documentation skills. Demonstrated commitment to continuous improvement and operational excellence. Education & Experience Bachelor's degree preferred, or an equivalent combination of education and at least three (2) years of customer service, receptionist, administrative support, front office, or human resources experience that demonstrates the knowledge, skills, and abilities required for this position. Experience in a customer-facing role requiring issue resolution, communication, and administrative support is strongly preferred. SALARY STRUCTURE: Hourly Pay Grade 4: $18.34 to $24.30 per hour SCHEDULE : In-person 8:30 am – 5:00 pm Monday - Friday; 38.75 hours per week"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Gardener_JR101281": {
      "title": "Gardener",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: Performs skilled gardening/horticultural tasks and manual work necessary to maintain university properties. QUALIFICATIONS: Strong verbal communication and listening skills Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills Highly thorough and dependable Practical knowledge and/or training in gardening and horticultural practices and procedures Practical knowledge and/or training in the pruning, trimming, watering fertilization and pest control of lawns, shrubs, trees, etc. Knowledge of the safe use and operation of hand and power tools used in groundskeeping Exercises behavior in accordance with university policies, the Facilities Missions and statement of Core Values; and complies with safety standards and demonstrates safe work habits Intermediate Virginia pesticide license, within 90 days Required:  Must have a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE:   H.S. Diploma or GED required Associate's degree Horticulture or related field preferred 2-4 years prior gardening and landscape maintenance and/or training at a college, university, county, city or commercial facility WORK HOURS: Full-time; non-exempt Monday-Friday 6:30AM-3:00PM, however, flexibility is required. All facilities employees are designated as essential personnel, are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are also subject to planned overtime work as required by the immediate supervisor SALARY STRUCTURE: Grade 3 Hourly ($17.61 to $21.13 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Ticketing-Services-Coordinator_JR101477": {
      "title": "Ticketing Services Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: Reporting to the Modlin Center for the Arts Patron Services Manager, the Ticketing Services Coordinator is a part-time, temporary position that provides management in the delivery of ticketing operations, online sales, and customer service. This position serves as the front-line box office representative and coordinator assisting in the sale of tickets for approximately 150 events per year in Modlin Center venues: Alice Jepson Theatre, Camp Concert Hall, Cousins Studio Theatre, and Perkinson Recital Hall, as well as other locations on- and off-campus. Events include performances, lectures, and special events involving both campus and community users and customers. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: General office environment Sitting Standing Able to lift up to 50 lbs QUALIFICATIONS:             Knowledge, skills & ability :  Experience with Spektrix ticketing platform (or similar CRM platform), including ticket sales, generating reports, and data management. Experience with customer service principles, practices, and procedures Excellent attention to detail and accuracy Outstanding communication, interpersonal, and reporting skills Ability to take initiative, anticipate needs, and provide recommendations on issues Strong commitment to supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and access             Education & experience :   Bachelor’s degree or combination of equivalent experience and training Two or more years of experience in customer service, ticketing services, or patron services, for an arts organization preferred Familiarity with industry standards in ticketing and box office management SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 3: $18.00 to $22.00 Scheduled Hours: Part-time 19 hours a week. Schedule may vary. Limited-term position from September to June."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Spider-Management-Intern_JR100973": {
      "title": "Spider Management Intern",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description Spider Management Company Summer Internship Opportunity for UR Partner Institutions ONLY: Virginia State University, Hampden-Sydney College, Randolph College, University of Lynchburg, The Citadel, Campbellsville University, Longwood University, PGA First Tee affiliation POSITION SUMMARY: Spider Management’s (Spider's) internship program is intended to expose a sophomore student to a wide range of strategies across the asset management industry. Spider Management Company is the investment office for the University of Richmond and a select group of other non-profit organizations. Spider’s 23-person team is split between Investments, Operations, and Investor Relations. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES : This internship will provide valuable hands-on experience across the organization’s three teams.  Projects could include, but are not limited to, research, data collection and analysis on potential investments, report production, database management, compliance, and market analysis.  Candidates will attend team meetings, as well as have the opportunity to attend meetings with external investment managers across asset classes (meetings may be virtual or in-person).  Candidates will also have the opportunity to take on ad-hoc projects to improve the team’s processes and/or explore select topics of interest for the purpose of presenting their findings to the team. Our intern program offers the following: The opportunity to immerse yourself in the daily responsibilities, projects, and initiatives Collaborate with seasoned professionals Interact with team members and senior leadership Gain exposure to various areas of the business Contribute to evaluations of prospective investment opportunities  What You Will Learn/Gain During the Internship: Basics of endowment and foundation investing Interact with and learn from world-class investment managers Fundamentals of asset allocation and portfolio management Introduction to multiple traditional and alternative investment asset classes Exposure to investment opportunities in a wide variety of sectors and geographies QUALIFICATIONS: Fall 2026 Sophomore , preferably majoring or minoring in Business/Finance or another quantitative discipline Must have excellent academic credentials Fully developed quantitative and analytical skills Proficiency in Microsoft Office products; advanced Excel and/or PowerPoint skills preferred Strong communication skills, both written and oral Ability to multi-task and work independently Detail-oriented and extremely proactive Self-starter High level of professionalism and integrity Cooperative attitude – must be a team player Interest in investment management industry, particularly non-profit investment management Must adhere to Spider Management Company’s Code of Ethics policy REQUIRED: Fall 2026 Sophomore Student; Econ and Finance majors strongly preferred Student attending a UR Partner Institution (see above if your institution is listed) Resume and cover letter Copy of Unofficial College Transcript Schedule: Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; schedule times may vary. Pay Rate: $20 to $25 an hour. This is an 8 to 10-week part-time, t emporary position, mid-May to mid-August 2027."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Visiting-Lecturer-in-Scenic-Design_JR101195": {
      "title": "Visiting Lecturer in Scenic Design",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Theatre & Dance in the School of Arts & Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a semester-long Visiting Lecturer in Scenic Design position for the spring semester, beginning on January 11, 2027. A Master of Fine Arts in Scenic Design/Scenography is required by the time of appointment. We seek a teacher-scholar who shares our commitment to highly effective teaching at all levels of the curriculum. The successful candidate will teach one course in scenic design (or a related area aligned with departmental needs); serve as Scenic Designer for our spring musical, Newsies (April 2027); design and oversee scenic properties for the production; work as lead scenic artist in close collaboration with our Technical Supervisor in the scene shop; and mentor and supervise student employees and lab students in the construction and painting process. Additional responsibilities include providing scenic support for our spring dance concert, including projection design depending on the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs, as well as collaborating on and mentoring student capstone projects with other faculty. The salary is competitive and commensurate with experience. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in properties, scenic painting, construction, and design techniques (e.g., hand drafting, Vectorworks, Photoshop, Qlab, and model-making / 3D visualization). Working knowledge of safety codes and practices, and of current trends in scenic technology. Experience working with diverse student populations and integrating/implementing inclusive pedagogies. Professional affiliations (e.g., USA829, USITT, regional, national, or international associations, etc.). Centering creative engagement at the crossroads of theatre, dance, and social justice, the department is committed to cultivating multiple perspectives, eliminating barriers to participation in the field, and, consequently, creating inclusive spaces and finding strength in our diversity. The successful candidate will not only share these values but will collaborate effectively with colleagues to build more inclusive and equitable theatre and dance through classes, rehearsals, and artistic processes. Applicants should apply online at  http://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae and a cover letter detailing their teaching philosophy and commitment to educating a diverse student body through inclusive pedagogies. In addition, please include copies of academic transcripts. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence immediately and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Professor White, Chair, mwhite5@richmond.edu, with any questions. For more information on the department and its programs, please visit https://theatredance.richmond.edu/ . The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $31,250. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-African-American-Literature_JR101479": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of African American Literature",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe University of Richmond Department of English invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. We seek a teacher-scholar committed to liberal arts education with primary expertise in African American literature. Any area of specialization is welcome, including, but not limited to, global Anglophone Black literature, poetics, film and media studies, creative nonfiction, environmental humanities, and/or disability studies. The successful applicant will be appointed in the English Department, engaging in the University of Richmond’s innovative programs in the humanities as well as intellectual and cultural initiatives across departments and schools. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain excellence in both teaching and research, and to engage in service as appropriate for their stage of career. Teaching load is 2/3, with small classes ranging from first-year seminars to advanced electives. Courses offered in the English Department may be considered for elective credit in the University of Richmond’s interdisciplinary programs. The successful candidate will have proven ability to foster an inclusive, student-centered learning environment through strong teaching and mentoring, a strong record of scholarly achievement, and clear promise for an impactful research agenda. Ph.D. in English or closely allied field in hand by August 2026 required. Online applications may be submitted at http://jobs.richmond.edu . Application materials should include a letter detailing the candidate’s research agenda and teaching interests, and discussing their commitment to creating an inclusive academic community, a curriculum vitae, and a writing sample of up to 25 pages. Candidates may also be asked, at a later date, to provide statements of research interests and teaching philosophy, as well as the names and contact information of three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Please address any questions about the position to the search committee chair, Nathan Snaza ( nsnaza@richmond.edu ). Applications should be received by October 1. The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $85,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Exhibition-Manager-and-Lead-Preparator_JR101213": {
      "title": "Exhibition Manager and Lead Preparator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Position Reposted. R eview of applications will begin on September 28, 2026 REQUIRED : Attach Resume and Cover Letter The Exhibition Manager and Lead Preparator for the University Museums oversees the planning, coordination, and implementation of all exhibitions and permanent collection installations at the Harnett Museum of Art and the Lora Robins Gallery. A critical member of the Museums' collaborative team and reporting to the Deputy Director and Curator of Exhibitions, the Exhibition Manager and Lead Preparator is responsible for managing the Museums' exhibition program, developing exhibition production timelines, budgets and installation plans, and ensuring exhibitions open on time and at the highest standards. A skilled art handler with broad experience in all media, the Exhibition Manager and Lead Preparator leads all exhibition preparation and fabrication and coordinates all audiovisual displays and new media in the Museums’ galleries. They supervise the Assistant Preparator, occasional contract workers, and UR student interns. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Work conditions are primarily inside, however, infrequent outside work will occur. Frequently required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to paint, solvents, adhesives, and air-borne particles, and to minimize risk while using specialty hand and power tools. Position requires ability to climb ladders; to have flexibility and ease of mobility; ability to lift and move large and heavy objects using basic moving equipment; and ability to stand for long periods of time. Position requires the ability to work unscheduled overtime during periods of installation/deinstallation and to support special programs and events. Position requires the use of personal vehicle. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:   Exhibition Planning and Management [40%] Oversees exhibition planning and management for all museum exhibitions and permanent collection installations in coordination with museum leadership Creates and develops exhibition and installation timelines using specialized project management software, including AirTable Ensures benchmarks are met according to schedule  Presents timelines to entire museum staff and assigns tasks as appropriate An expert in exhibition design, creates exhibition layouts professionally designed with CAD software, including in SketchUp, with assistance from Assistant Preparator and in consultation with curators  Ensures exhibitions and objects are installed according to the highest museum standards and best practices, and in accordance with programmatic, conservation, aesthetic and ADA requirements With Deputy Director and Director of Operations, prepares and administers exhibition budgets and estimates, and ensures that expenditures are within agreed upon budgetary limits  Researches, recommends and hires specialized contractors and specialized equipment as needed for complex exhibition projects and as approved by museum leadership Oversees and coordinates routine gallery inspections, touch-ups and cleaning  Exhibition Preparation and Installation [35%] Manages and executes all aspects of exhibition installation, deinstallation, and construction Prepares exhibition spaces in advance of exhibitions, schedules any necessary contractors or campus partners, coordinates gallery painting, and facilitates building all temporary walls and installation components  With museum curators, determines display casework and presentation furniture as needed for specific exhibitions, produces construction plans and shop drawings for all necessary furniture, and coordinates and builds all exhibition casework and display furniture as needed with Assistant Preparator Oversees the inventory and movement of cases, museum furniture, and other exhibition-related objects In consultation with curators, identifies framing and other artwork preparation needs for each exhibition Assigns and supervises artwork preparation, including artwork framing, by the Assistant Preparator Supervises the design, production, and installation of all exhibition didactics, text panels, title displays, object labels, and other graphic displays by the Assistant Preparator Well versed in the technical demands of time-based media and its installation, determines and coordinates all audiovisual requirements for exhibitions, assists with audiovisual equipment installation, set up and regular maintenance, and supervises any specialized contractors With Curator of Collections, coordinates delivery and handling activities for loaned artworks In consultation with museum curators, sets and maintains gallery lighting at accepted light levels With Director of Operations, manages vendors, temporary contractors and purchases, identifies skilled vendors and temporary contractors as needed, requests estimates, places orders, tracks, coordinates, and manages deliveries  Facilities Management [10%] Oversees facilities maintenance and upkeep, organization of collections and storage spaces, and museum workshops in coordination with the Director of Operations including scheduling and facilitating work with University Facilities and outside contractors Ensures that storage areas are maintained at professional levels of environmental and security control Monitors and maintains museum facilities, including overseeing the organization of collection storage spaces and maintaining the appearance of exhibition spaces by Exhibition Preparator Manages the museum workshop and establishes workshop standards to ensure overall safety of museum facilities Maintains tools and equipment and coordinates replacements with the Director of Operations Tracks inventories of tools, materials and supplies  Supervises the cleaning and organization of shop facility  Assists with museum facilities environmental monitoring Collections Care and Management [10%]  Ensures that collections storage areas are maintained at professional levels of environmental and security control and all collections objects are safely moved, stored and maintained In coordination with the Director of Operations, schedules and facilitates work with University Facilities and outside contractors Maintains and updates the location of collections objects, packing, materials, and supply storage with TMS Administrator Oversees and facilitates safe packing and transportation of collections objects  Supervises the matting and framing of collections objects and as well as the preparation of collection objects for storage and display by the Assistant Preparator In consultation with Museum leadership, curators, and TMS Administrator, makes conservation recommendations  With Curator of Collections, coordinates shipping and receiving, packing and unpacking of all collection objects to ensure proper handling and security procedures are followed  Supervision [5%] Supervises Assistant Preparator  Trains student interns and temporary exhibition workers Ensures all workers understand and implement best museum practices and safety protocols at all times in all museum spaces and workshops across campus.   QUALIFICATIONS:            Knowledge, skills & ability : Specialized knowledge of exhibition planning and project management, including project management software, such as AirTable. Highly skilled in exhibition design and recent exhibition technologies. Ability to easily translate curatorial ideas into 3-D models and CAD drawings. Broad knowledge of best museum practices for art handling and installation, collections management, object care, fabrication, and shipping. Fluent in time-based media installation. Highly skilled in object handling, carpentry, matting and framing, fabrication of exhibition furniture, platforms and displays, hanging and mounting systems, gallery lighting, moving objects, patching, painting, and proper ladder use and safety. Skilled and clear communicator who can work with a diverse range of colleagues and stakeholders, including museum leadership, university faculty and staff, visiting artists, and students. Ability to carefully assign tasks to ensure exhibition planning and installation is completed on time and on budget. Ability to make independent decisions and apply creative methods to solve complex installation problems with attention to detail. Ability to work in a diverse environment and with a diverse range of colleagues, stakeholders, and visitors is required. Ability to work on multiple projects and tasks simultaneously to meet deadlines. Ability to prioritize multiple projects and competing demands, balance tasks and see projects through to completion. Curious to continuously learn new skills and stay abreast of new developments in exhibition planning and design. Working knowledge of SketchUp, AirTable, The Gallery System (TMS) Collections Management Software, Adobe Creative Suite, and Microsoft Office Suite. Education & experience : Bachelor’s degree and at least 5 years of art preparator and exhibition planning and design experience with increasing responsibility. MFA is preferred Knowledge of museum best practices, an appreciation for the stewardship, safety and care, and preservation of fine art. Requires a successful driving check SALARY STRUCTURE: Full-time, with Benefits, Salaried, Exempt Pay Grade 6:   $50,461 to $66,851 Scheduled Hours: Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm, some nights and weekends"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Chef-de-Cuisine_JR101430-2": {
      "title": "Chef de Cuisine",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description **** For consideration, please submit a resume with your application**** POSITION SUMMARY : The Chef de Cuisine directs and bears accountability for all aspects of daily food production in Heilman Center for residential, commissary, special dietary and catering needs. As a member of the management team, the Chef de Cuisine assists the Executive Chef in executing the department’s vision, strategic priorities, and budget objectives, and assumes responsibility in the absence of the Executive Chef. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Operations: Directs and bears accountability for all aspects of daily food production and in Heilman Center for residential, commissary, Special Diet (FARE) and catering needs. Promotes and ensures excellence in food quality, presentation, and service; develops systems and processes. Maintains oversight of the menu management system on all platforms to ensure accuracy and optimum results. Maintains established health, safety, production, and service standards; establishes and maintains approved sanitation/HACCP codes and procedures. Collaborates with purchasing department to ensure accurate product specifications, menus, recipes, availability, and inventory. Monitors food purchases for integrity of ingredients. Ensures the maintenance and care of facilities, equipment, supplies, and materials. Works collaboratively with all Dining Services teams, as needed, to achieve department goals. Promotes the positive reputation of Dining Services and the University through effective public relations and communications with customers and staff. Assumes all responsibilities in the absence of Executive Chef. Executes cycle menu processes for Heilman Dining Center Executes Special event menu development Oversight of receiving team for all product ordering with daily operations and special events in Heilman Dining Supervision: Provides day-to-day leadership to the production chef manager team; provides coaching and guidance. This includes direct oversight of special diets operations for dining services Oversees compliance with all required logs, records, and files. Assesses, and ensures implementation, of staff training needs. Maintains oversight and development of culinary internship programs with external partners Administration Collaborates with executive chef on goals, menus, and events. Creates required financial and related reports as needed. Schedules staff and assigns duties as needed. CONTACTS:   This position interacts with faculty, staff, students, interns, visitors, guests, and vendors. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Staff (50+) WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT:   Designated essential personnel. Standard ADA Selection: Kitchen QUALIFICATIONS: Customer service Communications skills Flexibility skills Organizational/detail oriented Supervisory/leadership skills Analytical skills Math skills Technical skills Computer skills Comprehensive knowledge of all phases of high-volume food production, purchasing, menu planning, catering, and service. Strong knowledge of food and beverage controls. Must possess a current Virginia driver’s license with a driving record in good standing. CEC certification by the American Culinary Federation, or to be in current process and must be completed within two years HACCP certification within one year of hire. EDUCATION AND EXPERIENCE:  Degree in culinary arts and five years’ experience as a chef/culinary manager in a high-volume foodservice operation, required. Minimum of five years’ supervisory/management experience, required. Minimum of three years’ hands-on experience with menu management software, required. Combination of education and experience may be considered. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 8 ($68,474.00 - $101,858.00) WORK HOURS: Full Time, Exempt Sunday - Saturday, rotating weekends"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Cook-I_JR101431": {
      "title": "Cook I",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description JOB SUMMARY: As an entry level kitchen position working under direction, this position prepares food in quantity and is responsible for maintaining high standards for food quality while practicing proper sanitation, food safety, and cooking procedures. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Operations : (90%) • Cooks and prepares food as directed according to established recipes, special dietary or nutritional restrictions, or numbers of portions. Cooking may include display cooking • Cleans, cuts, and cooks all food products as assigned, including meat, fish, poultry, starches, and vegetables • Garnishes foods • Cleans, sets up, and breaks down kitchen equipment, appliances, and work areas to ensure cleanliness and functional operation • Operates kitchen, line equipment and machinery according to established safety procedures • Covers, labels, and dates all food products, and follows all food safety rules for proper storage • Monitors serving lines and ensures an adequate supply of food items, per the menu of the day, for the entire service period • Monitors and records temperatures of foods stored in refrigeration, in hot boxes, and on the serving line to ensure safe temperatures • Performs other duties, as assigned Additional Duties : (10%) • Maintains cleanliness of the productions areas • Washes pots, pans, dishes, utensils, and other cooking equipment, as needed CONTACTS : Contacts are primarily with direct supervisor and others in group or department to give and receive information. SUPERVISION EXERCISED : None WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT : Food services QUALIFICATIONS : Knowledge, skills & ability : • Ability to simultaneously handle multiple priorities • Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills • Possesses flexibility to work in a fast paced, dynamic environment • Friendly and service-oriented • Demonstrates a high commitment to quality • Excellent collaboration and team building skills Basic knowledge of culinary practices and techniques • Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices • Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire, required. Required dependent upon assigned unit Education & experience : • H.S. Diploma or GED required • One year of related work experience preferred Work Hours: Full Time, Non-Exempt Sunday - Saturday, PM hours (11:30am - 8:00pm, rotating weekends) Salary: Pay Grade 1 ($16.75/hour - $18.25/hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Compensation-Consultant_JR101448": {
      "title": "Compensation Consultant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Please submit a cover letter and resume POSITION SUMMARY: The Compensation Consultant provides compensation analysis, consultation and program support for the University’s compensation program. This position partners with the Director of Compensation and HR stakeholders to support job evaluation, market pricing, internal equity analysis, FLSA determinations, salary administration, compensation surveys, annual merit programs, and strategic compensation initiatives. KEY JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Partners with the Director of Compensation to administer, design, and maintain the University’s compensation and classification programs, ensuring alignment with compensation philosophy, market practices, internal equity, policies, and regulatory requirements. Performs job analysis and evaluation to determine appropriate classification, job level, salary range placement, and FLSA status within the University’s job framework. Provides guidance and recommendations on complex non-routine job evaluations and classification matters. Provides compensation consultation to Talent Acquisition, HR Business Partners, and organizational leaders on hiring, promotions, transfers, reorganizations, job leveling, and other compensation actions using market data, internal equity, and University pay policies. Partner with the Director to lead and develop compensation training as needed. Conducts market pricing, benchmarking, internal equity, compression, and compensation survey analyses to assess competitiveness, identify risks and trends, and develop recommendations for HR leadership; may provide guidance to junior analysts on day-to-day practices and compensation initiatives. Supports the annual salary and merit increase process, including administration, data validation, modeling, reporting, and compensation budgeting in partnership with HR, Finance, and Budget and Planning. In partnership with the Director, develops timeline, tools, and resources for key internal and external stakeholders to assist during the process. Monitors the effectiveness of existing compensation policies, programs, and practices and makes recommendations for enhancements and continuous improvement as needed. Develops compensation metrics, analytics, and reporting and translates complex data into actionable insights and recommendations for HR and senior leadership. Identifies trends, patterns, and potential compensation risks and opportunities through data analysis. Partners with Workday Sustainment, HRIS, and HR Operations to maintain compensation data, reporting, system configuration, data integrity, and accurate processing of compensation transactions. Provides compensation subject-matter expertise and guidance to Human Resources staff on job evaluation, market analysis, salary administration, policies, and complex compensation matters. Partners with Budget and Planning and other stakeholders to support position control, compensation budgeting, and position management processes. Partners with the Director of Compensation on strategic compensation initiatives, including job architecture, salary structure analysis, pay equity reviews, policy development, special studies, and process improvements. May be assigned to independently lead complex projects, analyses, or initiatives and present findings and recommendations to HR leadership and other stakeholders. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Office environment WORK HOURS: Full-time, exempt position Monday – Friday, 8:30 a.m. - 5:00 p.m.; 7.75 hrs./day; 38.75 hrs./week Hybrid work week after the first 90 days QUALIFICATIONS: Comprehensive understanding of compensation practices, programs, and plans Thorough understanding of federal and state laws and regulations Excellent analytical skills Technical proficiency using a human resources information system and Microsoft Office Products Demonstrated ability to maintain confidential information. Demonstrates a high level of accuracy, even under pressure. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE:  REQUIRED: Bachelor's degree and 3-5 years of relevant experience in compensation or a combination of education and experience from which comparable skills are acquired Preferred: Workday experience SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 9 (Hiring Range $73,008 to $121,659 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director--Marketing_JR101426-2": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Marketing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Job Summary: As an integral contributor to the Office of Admission, the Assistant Director is responsible for and contributes to initiatives to attract, recruit, enroll, and retain talented students from all backgrounds in support of the University and the Office of Admission’s missions and enrollment goals. The Assistant Director directly manages the development, creation, and execution of student search, recruitment, and marketing initiatives. The role includes significant copywriting across integrated marketing channels, collaboration on strategy and content for electronic correspondence and other marketing collateral. The Assistant Director also manages all aspects of recruitment within a designated geographic territory, and plans, implements, executes, and assesses admission activities and recruitment programs in that territory, representing the University to broad and diverse audiences as part of an innovative and highly collaborative team. Job Duties: Project Management and Contributions (50%) Contribute to the strategic development of an integrated student recruitment and communication plan for 400,000 prospects, 70,000 inquiries, and 17,000 applicants annually simultaneously for three enrollment cycles, including the creation of key and distinct branding messages, the timing and sequence of those messages, and what communication vehicles will most effectively influence college choice. Conduct strategic and tactical data analysis on recruitment, applicants, and yield marketing initiatives to recommend and implement innovative outreach strategies to all student and family constituencies. Support and collaborate on student search planning, development, and execution, including creative input on marketing messages and involvement in student search strategy to build the top of enrollment funnel. Contribute to the creation of brand-aligned content and serve as primary copywriter across channels including print, web, video, electronic correspondence, and other media, specifically in support of student search and marketing initiatives. Prospect Management Responsibilities and General Recruitment Activities (50%) Build relationships with prospective students and families and serve as the primary point of contact for designated geographic territory. Counsel and engage with students to determine the students’ educational interests and needs and align those interests and needs with the attributes and strengths of the University. Establish and cultivate and sustain relationships with school and community-based organization counselors within a specific recruiting territory. Establish short- and long-range prospect management goals and action plans for individually assigned geographic regions. Develop expertise for individually designated geographic territory using various data to determine the recruitment goals and strategies for the region. Plan and implement admission activities related to the recruitment and yield of prospective students from an individually designated geographic territory, including planning all travel logistical arrangements and coordinating high school visits in that region, and planning regional receptions, counselor workshops and/or off-campus interviews. Determine the recruitment and yield activities that are most effective for designated geographic region and develop a plan with justifications for selected activities; manage the travel budget for expenditures associated with off-campus recruitment and yield activities. Review applications and participate in Admission Committee in the contextual holistic review of the academic and personal qualifications of candidates for both admission and merit scholarship consideration. Serve as a representative of the University to a broad and diverse audience at college fairs, high school programs, community-based organization programs, information sessions, and regional receptions. Create and deliver informational and compelling messages that address the educational needs of various groups.  Develop and innovate new presentation concepts and ideas as appropriate for evolving audiences. Responsible for other duties as assigned to support University and Office of Admission goals. Contacts: Perform job duties as part of a collaborative team. Collaborate with intra-office and campus-wide offices and teams, including Financial Aid, Scholars and Fellowships, Slate Solutions, The Hub for Student Inclusion and Community, Student Development, University Communications, Information Services, and Housing. Represent the University and, when possible, take on leadership roles at regional and national professional conferences to highlight the University of Richmond, strengthen brand recognition, and to remain abreast of enrollment best practices to support innovation and strong recruitment and admission programming. Partner with cross-campus colleagues to support an inclusive, positive, and distinctive recruitment process. Liaise with secondary school counselors, independent counselors, advising and community-based organizations, and prospective students and families to provide accurate and authentic information about the University of Richmond. Supervision Exercised: Hire, train, supervise, and mentor admission staff and/or student employees to support admission initiatives that positively impact enrollment goals. Model positive and professional example in all interactions. Support and imbue an understanding of overall Admission strategic goals among supervisees. Working Conditions/Physical Effort: This position requires travel and working weekends or evenings for special programs at peak times throughout the year. Each year the Office of Admission visits more than 950 high schools and attends college fairs in over 30 states, Washington, D.C., and 20+ countries. This position requires lifting supplies / materials of up to 30 pounds. Qualifications: Knowledge, skills & ability Persuasive communication skills, including excellent public speaking skills; strong written and interpersonal communication skills. Demonstrated commitment to supporting access to selective university admission for high performing, talented historically underserved student populations. Experience in supporting inclusion and a sense of belonging within a diverse community of students, faculty and staff. A thorough understanding of and appreciation for the selective, private college admission process. Strong industry knowledge and commitment to staying current on national and international student markets and mobility trends. Demonstrated ability to utilize data to assess, evaluate, and validate decisions. Highly self-motivated, productive, and adaptable. Creative and independent critical thinker with advanced problem-solving capabilities and an innovative and collaborative approach. Leads with a positive attitude and strong work ethic. Demonstrated success working collaboratively with various internal and external constituencies. The ability to handle highly confidential information is required; prior experience working with FERPA protected data is preferred. Ability to prioritize and manage multiple tasks concurrently coupled with excellent organizational skills with highest standards for accuracy. Familiarity with software platforms such as Excel and Slate CRM. Must have a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing. Must have a valid passport and the ability to travel internationally. Must be willing, able and eager to travel up to four weeks each semester. Flexible to adapt to other duties as assigned to support the Office of Admission and the University mission and goals. Education & Experience Bachelor’s degree required A minimum of three years of professional experience in college admission, school counseling, or enrollment management, including responsibilities for domestic and international recruitment, travel strategy and management, and selective admission application review. Fluency or proficiency in second language preferred. Work hours: Full-time, in-person, exempt position Monday – Friday, 8:30 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Evening and weekend hours are required. Salary Structure: Grade 6 ($50,461 to $66,851 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Asst-Athletics-Director-for-Annual-Giving_JR101436": {
      "title": "Asst Athletics Director for Annual Giving",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: Please submit resume and cover letter The Assistant Athletics Director for Annual Giving is responsible for the overall management and strategic planning of athletics annual giving, consisting of annual contributions designated towards the Athletics Annual Fund and discretionary funds for each intercollegiate sport. The Assistant Director of Athletics for Annual Giving reports to the Associate Director of Athletics for Development. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: No or very limited exposure to physical risk. limited physical effort required. Position involves regular and frequent travel/out of office appointments Willingness to work frequent evenings and weekend in conjunction with university events QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability :  Excellent interpersonal and leadership skills, along with the ability to work with diverse internal and external constituencies Strong organizational skills and attention to detail, in conjunction with the comfort level of working in a fast-paced environment while handling multiple projects required Demonstrated strong written and oral communication skills Proven ability to work effectively in a collaborative, team environment Experience working with and managing volunteers preferred Personal solicitation experience required Skill in use of personal computers, standard office software applications, and fundraising software, with an understanding of database management  Ability to maintain confidentiality Valid driver's license with a good 3-year driving history Understanding and passion for collegiate athletics    Mature judgment    Education & experience :  Bachelor's degree required Minimum five years’ experience in annual giving, or a closely related field preferably in higher education, with a proven track record of success Schedule Hours: Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; 38.75 hours per week Salary Grade 9 (Exempt): $ 77,854- $ 107,058"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Creative-Content_JR101421": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Creative Content",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: The Assistant Director of Creative Content coordinates all creative content activities relevant to his/her assigned sports, including videography, photography, and graphic design. This person serves as the primary visual storyteller for coaches and student-athletes and works continually to promote the image of Richmond Athletics, using available data and analytics to guide content strategy and resource allocation. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Serve as the primary creative content contact for men’s basketball and at least two additional sports assigned by supervisor by   directing graphic design, video production, photography, and social media operations. Sport assignments are subject to change. (70%) Produce original recruiting materials highlighting assigned sports programs and what they offer prospective student-athletes. (10%) Maintain organized, manageable visual archives (photo/video) for all assigned sports. (10%) Promote additional Athletics Department initiatives, such as leadership curriculum, academic programs, and special events, through original videography, photography, and visual storytelling. (10%) CONTACTS: Coaching staffs and student-athletes – to film or photography practices, games, and other team events. Athletic staff from other schools and conferences – to obtain access to away events and share footage and photos. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Assist with the day-to-day supervision of graduate and undergraduate student assistants. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Some physical demand of transporting equipment for game management at athletic events is required (cameras, tripods, lighting, etc.). Travel with teams as assigned to produce creative content for RichmondSpiders.com and social media, including, video and photographs. Scheduled Hours:   Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; 38.75 hours per week. Some weekends and evenings. Travel is required. QUALIFICATIONS: Possess athletic creative content experience, including, photography, videography, and design, at the college or professional level. Excellent communications skills; Individual should have working knowledge of Mac operating system, video operation and editing software such as Adobe Creative Cloud (Premiere Pro, AfterEffects, Lightroom, Photoshop). Adherence to all applicable NCAA, conference, and institutional rules and regulations is required. Education and experience: Bachelor’s degree required; a master’s degree is preferred. 1-3 years in college or professional athletic creative content. Salary Structure: Pay Grade 5 (Salary): $43,867 to $58,136 SCHEDULE: Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm, some nights, weekends and travel"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/CDO-Operations-Specialist_JR101433": {
      "title": "CDO Operations Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description ***For consideration, please submit a COVER LETTER and RESUME *** POSITION SUMMARY:  The Career Development Office (CDO) Operations Specialist oversees the strategic operations, complex coordination and professional execution of a diverse set of responsibilities integral to the creation and delivery of law student and alumni career and professional development services. The Operations Specialist manages the physical space within which the CDO is located, greets all visitors, ensures a professional and warm environment and works regularly with constituents including students, alumni, legal employers, and law school faculty and administration. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Office Management (30%) Provides and ensures continuous in-person reception area and telephone coverage during business hours. Greets and directs visitors, answers and triages telephone calls, manages multiple email inboxes and calendars, and processes incoming mail. Oversees and maintains physical space and identifies and secures outside space as needed, to support the CDO’s mission Initiates and tracks office and equipment maintenance requests.  Conducts environmental scanning to determine constituent pain points and frequently-asked-questions from visitors and shares that information with advising team to improve services. Develops and executes strategies to improve student engagement and the in-office experience for CDO visitors.  Evaluates, purchases, and maintains career development resources. Selects, negotiates pricing, controls inventory and purchases supplies and promotional products (for employers and alumni) from vendors.  Coordinates projects and priorities with other Law School departments and the University.  Performs research, investigation, and coordination of special projects related to career and professional development as assigned by the Associate Dean.  Program and Event Management (30%) Designs, educates faculty and students, and manages the process for coordination and submission of judicial clerkship applications, including exercising independent judgment to customize confidential faculty letters of recommendation. Coordinates listings, applicants’ data, and documents between 12Twenty (LawQuest) and the Online System for Clerkship Applications and Review (OSCAR). Performs Law School’s annual OSCAR system maintenance. Maintains confidential student information and Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) documentation. Develops process and application for annual Summer Public Service Fellowship Program (SPSFP) in coordination with internal and external partners. Interprets SPSFP requirements for students and employers using independent judgment. Manages faculty-led SPSFP discussion groups for all participants. Executes logistics for large-scale events for students, alumni and employers—including segments of new student orientation programs, small-group workshops, mock interviews, interviews, networking events, and receptions, in collaboration with the Associate Director for Career & Professional Development Programs. Tracks RSVPs, coordinates day-of logistics, and follows up with participants post-event. Collaborates with team to manage logistics for student advisory board meetings and projects. Communicates regularly with members and develops meeting agendas. Collaborates with the advising team to develop CDO policies and procedures while maintaining compliance with American Bar Association standards, National Association for Law Placement (NALP) principles and best practices. Schedules, assigns and communicates with students about 1L and 2L career advising required sessions. Supports law school staff and faculty in the use 12Twenty (LawQuest). Creates and delivers customized reports. Communications (20%) Drafts, edits and maintains CDO signage, including flyers, posters, dedicated bulletin board and electronic message boards. Communicates with judges, law firm partners, corporate executives, government officials, alumni and other high-profile employer contacts regarding their recruiting needs.  Budget and Financial Management (20%) Collaborates with the Associate Dean on strategic planning for resource allocation and makes annual budget recommendations based on analysis of past spending. Manages all CDO budget accounts, including reviewing and reconciling expenses. Initiates, negotiates, and tracks departmental contracts. Enters new agreements into the University’s contract management system. Resolves invoice and payment issues directly with vendors. Develops and delivers custom budget reports. CONTACTS: External Constituents: Law alumni and legal employers, including law firms, government and public interest organizations, corporations, and judges—to facilitate meeting their recruiting needs and/or to coordinate their participation in CDO professional development programs. Career development teams at other schools – to facilitate our participation in consortia programs, secure reciprocity for our students and/or share information regarding best practices. Law School faculty, administration and staff: Law school colleagues—to facilitate CDO events and operations, share information regarding students and alumni and learn more about law school initiatives of interest to current and prospective employers. Other University departments: Departments including Events, Communications, Facilities, Catering/Dining, Budget Office, Payroll, Student Accounts, Student Employment, Printing Services, Information Services, Parking Services, and Human Resources, among others – to facilitate CDO events and operations. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: The Operations Specialist will collaborate with the Assistant Director of Recruiting to train student employees. The Operations Specialist may supervise temporary administrative employees from time-to-time.  WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT:  The Operations Specialist will work in person in an office environment 100% of the time. They must be able to lift and carry up to 25 pounds. Standing for short periods also is required. Some night and weekend work, and some travel are required, particularly for career programs, special events, or external trainings. QUALIFICATIONS: Keen attention to detail and the ability to maintain accuracy while working through interruptions. Strong oral and written communications skills. Excellent organizational, project-management, problem-solving, interpersonal, and customer service skills.  Strong judgment and the ability to work effectively independently and as part of a team. Ability to perform well in a fast-paced professional setting. Discretion in handling sensitive and confidential information. Knowledge of finance, accounting, budgeting and cost control procedures. Understanding of office maintenance principles and procedures, including services, equipment, supplies and inventory control. Flexibility and a sense of humor. Competency in Microsoft Office (especially Word, Outlook Excel, and PowerPoint); Banner; Banner Web; EMS; Cascade; MailChimp; Constant Contact; and 12Twenty software (or facility with web-based databases and contact management software generally). Education & experience :   Required : Bachelor’s degree, plus 3 - 5 years of administrative experience in a fast-paced environment. Project management experience. Preferred : Experience in a legal environment to include law practice, legal education or legal recruiting/hiring. Pay Grade: 5  Hourly Full-time with Benefits, Non-exempt Rate $21.09 to $27.95 SCHEDULED Hours: Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; 38.75 hours per week."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Custodial-Floor-Technician_JR101438": {
      "title": "Custodial Floor Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: The Custodial Floor Technician maintains the standards of cleanliness in accordance with operating procedures and adheres to all other University of Richmond policies and guidelines. The Custodial Floor Technician also upholds the Facilities Department’s mission which is to create and maintain an outstanding learning environment through constant attention to our community’s needs and thoughtful care of beautiful campus.  RESPONSIBILITIES: Advocates safety for everyone on campus by identifying, reporting and addressing when appropriate Interacts daily with diverse staff, students and faculty by providing a welcoming and positive atmosphere Preserves the heritage and unique infrastructure of the University by exceeding industry cleanliness standards Cleans assigned areas as directed and follows operating procedures Responsible for the care and upkeep of resources provided to them by the University Operates various types of equipment used for floor care maintenance such as automatic scrubbers, wet/dry vacuums, carpet extractors, burnishers, etc. Performs various tasks including but not limited to mopping, burnishing, stripping, waxing, refinishing, scrubbing and cleaning different floor types. Responds and assists with any custodial related requests as directed WORKING HOURS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Must be able to lift, push and pull up to 50 pounds Must be able to properly operate floor care equipment Must be able to stand for long periods of time, go up and down stairs and walk for long periods Must be able to work outdoors under adverse weather conditions including heat, rain and snow Frequently required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to hazardous materials QUALIFICATIONS: Minimum of a High School degree or educational equivalent preferred One year or more of experience in custodial operations or a related field preferred Professional experience in floor care preferred Ability to effectively communicate with listed contacts above Required: possess a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing WORK HOURS: Full-time, non-exempt position  Monday-Friday 6:00am-2:30pm with some weekend hours required All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are subject to planned overtime work as required by their immediate supervisor. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 2 (Hiring Range $16.01 to $19.21 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Sales-Associate_JR101392-1": {
      "title": "Part-Time Sales Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: As the point of first contact for UR Spider Shop customers this position is responsible for providing customer service, assistance with daily store operations, and cash handling responsibilities. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Customer Service and Store Operations : (50%) Performs all routine functions one would normally find in a retail operation, including: Maintains presence on sales floor, assisting customers and staff with merchandise information Provides information to patrons regarding pertinent rules, policies, and procedures related to cash and cash related transactions; directs patrons to proper offices, as appropriate, to seek resolution of problems beyond the information available to the cashier Assists with merchandising and stocking merchandise Participates as a member of the bookstore team in ensuring that all areas of the store are neat and clean, supports and staffs store events as needed, attends team meetings and participates in bookstore projects as requested Answers telephones Trains employees, as needed Additional related duties as assigned Cash Handling : (50%) Operates point-of-sale system. Accurately rings up sales using a variety of payments; makes correct change; issues receipts Verifies cash drawers Organizes daily receipts paperwork (cash deposits and reports) Complies with all established standards for handling cash, credit, records, and the like. Ensures confidentiality of university and customer records Processes paperwork and billing, as needed QUALIFICATIONS: Demonstrated analytical skills Demonstrated customer service skills Maintains a high degree of professionalism Working knowledge of retail operations environment and point-of-sale systems General knowledge of cash management principles and procedures; ability to comply with cash/credit compliance standards General computer knowledge and ability to learn new software Education & experience :  H.S. Diploma or GED required 2 years of experience cashiering preferred 1 year of experience in retail customer service preferred WORK HOURS: Part-time; non-exempt; temporary position (will work no more than 6 months). Schedule varies based on department needs but will work no more than 19 hours per week. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 1 (Hiring Range $15.00 to $17.79 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Asst-Coach-Cross-Country-and-Track-and-Field_JR100603": {
      "title": "Asst Coach Cross Country and Track and Field",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : The Assistant Coach, Cross Country and Track and Field is responsible for assisting with all phases of the Division I Cross Country and Track and Field programs at the University of Richmond. Specific responsibilities include but are not limited to: coaching and student-athlete skill development, recruiting, administrative tasks related to the operation of the program, and providing a positive collegiate experience for student-athletes. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Regular travel is required (recruiting and team travel) QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to lead/manage others Effective interpersonal skills Strong verbal communication and listening skills Strong understanding of all aspects of social media is preferred Demonstrated knowledge of all applicable NCAA, conference, and institutional rules and regulations is required Demonstrated expertise in coaching Cross Country/Track and Field is required Must have a valid driver's license for at least 3 years and a clean driving record, required EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE :  Bachelor's Degree required Master's Degree preferred 2-4 years previous experience Previous coaching on the collegiate level preferred WORK HOURS : Monday to Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; 38.75 hours per week. Travel required SALARY STRUCTURE : Pay Grade AC (Hiring Range $23,660.00 to $61,830.00 annually/Budget for position between $45,000 and $50,000)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Asst-Director--Admissions-Communications--Print-_JR101404": {
      "title": "Asst Director, Admissions Communications (Print)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description JOB SUMMARY: As an integral contributor to the Office of Admission, the Assistant Director is responsible for and contributes to initiatives to attract, recruit, enroll, and retain talented students from all backgrounds in support of the University and the Office of Admission’s missions and enrollment goals. The Assistant Director directly manages the development, creation, and execution of all admission print materials along with mailing strategy and execution. The role includes significant copywriting across integrated marketing channels, including collaboration on strategy and content for electronic correspondence and other marketing collateral. The Assistant Director also manages all aspects of recruitment within a designated geographic territory, and plans, implements, executes, and assesses admission activities and recruitment programs in that territory, representing the University to broad and diverse audiences as part of an innovative and highly collaborative team JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Project Management and Contributions (50%) Contribute to the strategic development of an integrated student recruitment and communication plan for 400,000 prospects, 70,000 inquiries, and 17,000 applicants annually simultaneously for multiple enrollment cycles, including the creation of key and distinct branding messages, the timing and sequence of those messages, and what communication vehicles will most effectively positively influence college choice. Plan, implement, and serve as the creative and project lead for all recruitment, applicants, and yield print materials. Lead office liaison for the creative development and execution of all publications with University Communications and third-party vendors. Contribute strategic insights to brand-aligned content for the office across channels including print, web, video, electronic correspondence, and other media. Assist with the annual development and implementation of the office’s strategic marketing and communication plan.   Liaises with the Office of Institutional Effectiveness to verify admission data to ensure accurate information about the University is distributed to on- and off- campus audiences. Prospect Management Responsibilities and General Recruitment Activities (50%) Build relationships with prospective students and families and serve as the primary point of contact for designated geographic territory. Counsel and engage with students to determine the students’ educational interests and needs and align those interests and needs with the attributes and strengths of the University. Establish and cultivate and sustain relationships with school and community-based organization counselors within a specific recruiting territory. Establish short- and long-range prospect management goals and action plans for individually assigned geographic regions. Develop expertise for individually designated geographic territory using various data to determine the recruitment goals and strategies for the region. Plan and implement admission activities related to the recruitment and yield of prospective students from an individually designated geographic territory, including planning all travel logistical arrangements and coordinating high school visits in that region, and planning regional receptions, counselor workshops and/or off-campus interviews. Determine the recruitment and yield activities that are most effective for designated geographic region and develop a plan with justifications for selected activities; manage the travel budget for expenditures associated with off-campus recruitment and yield activities. Review applications and participate in Admission Committee in the contextual holistic review of the academic and personal qualifications of candidates for both admission and merit scholarship consideration. Serve as a representative of the University to a broad and diverse audience at college fairs, high school programs, community-based organization programs, information sessions, and regional receptions. Create and deliver informational and compelling messages that address the educational needs of various groups.  Develop and innovate new presentation concepts and ideas as appropriate for evolving audiences. Responsible for other duties as assigned to support University and Office of Admission goals. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Hire, train, supervise, and mentor admission staff and/or student employees to support admission initiatives that positively impact enrollment goals. Model positive and professional example in all interactions. Support and imbue an understanding of overall Admission strategic goals among supervisees. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: This position requires travel and working weekends or evenings for special programs at peak times throughout the year. Each year the Office of Admission visits more than 950 high schools and attends college fairs in over 30 states, Washington, D.C., and 20+ countries. QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability Persuasive communication skills, including excellent public speaking skills; strong written and interpersonal communication skills. Demonstrated commitment to supporting access to selective university admission for high performing, talented historically underserved student populations. Experience in supporting inclusion and a sense of belonging within a diverse community of students, faculty and staff. A thorough understanding of and appreciation for the selective, private college admission process. Strong industry knowledge and commitment to staying current on national and international student markets and mobility trends. Demonstrated ability to utilize data to assess, evaluate, and validate decisions. Highly self-motivated, productive, and adaptable. Creative and independent critical thinker with advanced problem-solving capabilities and an innovative and collaborative approach. Leads with a positive attitude and strong work ethic. Demonstrated success working collaboratively with various internal and external constituencies. The ability to handle highly confidential information is required; prior experience working with FERPA protected data is preferred. Ability to prioritize and manage multiple tasks concurrently coupled with excellent organizational skills with highest standards for accuracy. Familiarity with software platforms such as Excel and Slate CRM. Must have a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing. Must have a valid passport and the ability to travel internationally. Must be willing, able and eager to travel up to four weeks each semester. Flexible to adapt to other duties as assigned to support the Office of Admission and the University mission and goals. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Bachelor’s degree required A minimum of three years of professional experience in college admission, school counseling, or enrollment management, including responsibilities for domestic and international recruitment, travel strategy and management, and selective admission application review. Fluency or proficiency in second language preferred. WORK HOURS: Full-time, in-person, exempt position Monday – Friday, 8:30 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. This position requires travel and working weekends or evenings for special programs at peak times throughout the year. SALARY STRUCTURE: Grade 6 ($50,461 to $66,851 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Music-Technology-Specialist_JR101381-1": {
      "title": "Music Technology Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY:  The Music Technology Specialist is responsible for managing the music technology lab (15-station Mac lab), project studios (three audio recording and post-production studios), as well as supervising student employees, managing equipment sign-out, conducting workshops, recording and post-production of concerts, live-streaming concerts, training and supervision of student workers, researching appropriate music technologies, maintaining equipment, supporting Third Practice Music Festival, and other music concert production. QUALIFICATIONS: Attention to detail and strong communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills Experience and proficiency in recording including digital audio workstations (Ableton Live, Logic, Pro Tools), recording hardware including consoles, preamps, microphones. Knowledge of appropriate microphone choice and effective microphone placement. Experience in audio post-production including effective and appropriate use of plugins Experience live-streaming audio and video Knowledge of and experience managing Macintosh computers including updating operating systems and installing software. Basic video editing skills and experience Basic knowledge of Dante audio networking Strong interpersonal skills and a service-minded approach to interaction with students, faculty, staff, and departmental visitors Ability to prioritize work assignments amidst frequent interruptions Ability to take initiative on long-term or recurring assignments Proficiency in Excel, Word, and Outlook. Knowledge of Adobe Acrobat Pro or willingness to learn these or other software needed to perform job assignments.  Ability to learn to use Workday, Blackboard, and Box Knowledge of and experience with HTML and CSS Ability to manage student workers, equipment sign-out, music labs and classrooms; to conduct ongoing research and develop justifications for equipment proposals; and to develop/teach technology-based workshops on an ongoing basis. Ability to read, interpret, and apply University and departmental policies and procedures EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: BA or BM in music composition, music technology or other field within the digital/media arts preferred. Prior work experience preferred but not mandatory. WORK HOURS: Full-time, non exempt position This position is limited term for one year with fulltime benefits (extension possible with approval) Monday – Friday, 8:30 a.m. - 5:00 p.m.; 7.75 hrs./day; 38.75 hrs./week; may require evening and weekend work for concert recording and production support  Hourly Grade 4: $18.34 to $30.26"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assoc-AD-Compliance_JR101400-1": {
      "title": "Assoc AD Compliance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: The Associate AD/Compliance independently coordinates, plans, organizes and implements all monitoring systems of the Athletics Compliance program, along with developing and implementing comprehensive and continuing NCAA and conference rules-education programs and procedures.  The position also plays a strong role in enforcing University, conference, and NCAA regulations, to include all independent investigations and recommendations to Sr Assoc. AD/Chief of Staff of noncompliance. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Monitors recruiting activities (20%) On a daily basis verifies that all coaches have accurate records regarding recruiting telephone calls, correspondence, and contacts/evaluations. Independently checks above records for compliance with NCAA Bylaw 13 and makes judgments as to whether there was a reportable violation. Responsible for ensuring coaches’ recruiting travel is compliant with NCAA rules by cross-checking travel expense reports and recruiting logs to ensure activities took place during appropriate recruiting periods and within overall NCAA limits. Delivers interpretations of Bylaw 13 on which coaches rely and off of which they base decisions. Develops and enforces new monitoring strategies on an ongoing basis. Compiles rules violation reports for Senior Associate Athletic Director/Chief of Staff which include facts/findings and recommendations for penalties/sanctions and communicates penalties/sanctions to appropriate staff. Monitors financial aid (20%) Identifies student-athletes that appear to be non-counters and generates the necessary non-counter certification paperwork to be reviewed by the Financial Aid office and approved by the Faculty Athletic Representative. Tracks compliance with NCAA Bylaw 15’s financial aid regulations by inputting all financial aid information into the Compliance Assistant Internet (CAi) software, generating squad lists, and reviewing with Registrar and Financial Aid Office. Develops and implements new monitoring strategies on an ongoing basis. Name, Image and Likeness (20%) Manage the department’s efforts surrounding regulatory changes which allow student-athletes to benefit from the use of their name, image, and likeness. Manages the name, image, and likeness compliance and rules education operations due to the newly created rules surrounding name, image, and likeness impacting student-athletes. Coordinates NIL training and coordinates internal and external partners to execute a broad-based NIL program. Leads the Department’s NIL strategy and empowering student-athletes to maximize their impact in the areas of professionalism, financial growth, and NIL.  Develops and delivers best in class NIL-specific education for student-athletes and Athletics staff. Administers the institution's compliance responsibilities under College Sports Commission (CSC) regulations, including oversight of NIL Go disclosures, monitoring reporting deadlines and requirements, and coordinating with student-athletes and institutional stakeholders to ensure timely and accurate submissions. Serves as the department's primary administrator for the CSC CAPS platform, managing roster and benefits reporting, annual attestations, and other required institutional submissions to ensure compliance with CSC regulations and applicable NCAA legislation. Independently develops and implements systems to track student-athlete NIL activity.  This includes providing rules education to both student-athletes, staff, and external constituencies. Monitors playing and practice season activities (10%) Monitors each sport’s playing and practice season calendar to ensure compliance with NCAA rules pertaining to NCAA Bylaw 17.  Delivers interpretations of Bylaw 17 on which coaches rely and off of which they base decisions. Develops and implements new monitoring strategies on an ongoing basis. Other ongoing risk management and monitoring duties (which include, but are not limited to) (15%) Point person within the Athletic Compliance Office for the planning, advertising, and administration of all sports camps and clinics to ensure compliance with all NCAA rules and regulations. Independently develops and implements systems to track student-athlete employment.  This includes providing rules education to both student-athletes and potential employers and monitoring that employment for compliance with NCAA rules and regulations. Administers participation lists that track seasons of competition utilized by each student-athlete. Monitors student-athlete participation in outside competition and outside leagues both during the academic year and over vacation periods to ensure such participation will not have a negative impact on the student-athlete’s intercollegiate eligibility. Assists the Department of Athletics in managing regulatory changes. Develops rules education content and serves as primary liaison with athletics compliance technology provider. Provides NCAA rules interpretations to coaches, student-athletes and staff. NCAA Training (5%) Assists in conducting monthly compliance meetings with the coaching staffs in all sports to provide rules education and discuss monitoring strategies. Conducts training sessions with all Olympic sport coaching staffs targeting NCAA compliance and new rules as instituted by NCAA. Conducts investigations of non-compliance by gathering information of potential violations. This includes compiling documentation of alleged violations from staff and/or student-athletes.   Student-Athlete Conduct (5%) Works to manage and process student-athlete conduct issues on behalf of the Department of Athletics. Collaborates with the Dean’s Office, Housing and Residence Life, and Athletics Administration to manage student-athlete conduct issues. Performs other duties as assigned by the Sr. Associate AD/Chief of Staff (5%) CONTACTS : The Associate AD/Compliance will interact regularly with a variety of constituencies including student-athletes, prospective student-athletes, coaches, athletics and university staff, and parents of student-athletes and prospective student-athletes.  The purpose of such contact will be to provide rules education and to provide interpretive support by answering complex questions related to the application of NCAA legislation. QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability :  Must have excellent written and verbal communication skills, the ability to perform accurate detail-oriented work, and the ability to communicate effectively with a diverse population.  Must possess the ability to maintain confidentiality and demonstrate a commitment to integrity. Must possess the analytical and problem-solving skills necessary to interpret and apply the complex and ever-changing NCAA rules and regulations. Advanced knowledge of the complex NCAA rules with the ability to interpret those rules and applicable NCAA investigative methods. Ability to educate and train staff on NCAA compliance standards. Required to have an active driver's license for the last 3 years; may be required to drive university vehicles Education & experience : Bachelor’s degree required; Masters or Law Degree preferred 2 – 4 years NCAA Division I compliance experience preferred Salary Grade 7 Exempt: $58,011 to $86,299 Scheduled Hours:    Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; 38.75 hours per week"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Digital-Content-Creator_JR101364": {
      "title": "Digital Content Creator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description A cover letter is recommended when applying for this position. JOB SUMMARY: The Digital Content /Creator Specialist serves as a writer and content creator for the University of Richmond’s flagship digital publications, with particular emphasis on University of Richmond Magazine and URNow , the institution’s weekly outbound news publication. The position partners with colleagues in University Communications and beyond to create, deploy, and leverage multi-channel and cross-channel content that drives key business objectives and optimizes content for search and generative engines. The position also assists with the logistics and strategy of content management and distribution. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Content Creation and Management (65%) Serve as a key writer/editor/content manager for flagship digital publications that build engagement and awareness among key audiences and drive institutional objectives Create content for social, social search, and web properties to effectively tell the University's story and authentically demonstrate the Richmond experience Contribute to a range of cross-platform projects and initiatives led by University Communications, including print counterparts to digital publications Develop and/or contribute to strategic editorial approaches for existing and new projects Assist with the integration of brand-based messaging in digital content Project management (20%) Manage website content for select publications and platforms Create and distribute institutional emails and other digital content Ensure content creation and distribution meet deadlines and strategic objectives Ensure the quality of digital and print deliverables through proofreading, fact-checking, and other related functions Review and report on defined metrics/KPIs that measure the effectiveness of digital publications and content Manage sensitive materials and information with discretion Campus Partnerships (10%) Build and maintain excellent relationships with campus partners to understand their goals and engage them in strategically chosen, collaborative storytelling and content opportunities Other (5%) Participate in concept development and project critiques with the communications team Other duties as assigned WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Environment: Office Exposures encountered: Standard office environment Physical effort: Sedentary, light lifting required on occasion Very little travel expected, other than possible professional development opportunities QUALIFICATIONS: Exceptional ability to write compelling stories and create brand-based narratives across a range of digital communications platforms Excellent ability to create effective content across a wide variety of media and project types Excellent interpersonal, communications, and listening skills Excellent writing, editing, grammar, and proofreading skills Outstanding attention to detail Outstanding strategic-thinking and problem-solving skills Strong ability to think creatively Strong knowledge of AP style. Working knowledge of Chicago and MLA styles Strong competency in current software tools and applications (including Adobe Creative Suite, Microsoft Suite, web/digital/social content platforms, etc.) Strong experience with digital content management Proven ability to exercise discretion and independent judgment Demonstrated knowledge of social/social search platforms and best practices Demonstrated experience capturing and using data analytics to inform content strategy Demonstrated understanding of search engine optimization (SEO) and generative engine optimization (GEO) principles and best practices Demonstrated experience thriving in a creative environment Demonstrated ability to work effectively with, and build consensus among, people at all levels of the organization Demonstrated ability to collaborate, build consensus, and work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community Demonstrated ability to work independently as well as within a team setting Demonstrated ability to provide and receive constructive criticism in a productive manner Demonstrated ability to handle multiple tasks simultaneously, coupled with the ability to set and recommend priorities Demonstrated ability to handle quick turnaround times EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Bachelor's degree in English, communications, journalism, digital content creation, or related field is required A minimum of three years of experience in digital content creation, writing, magazines, marketing communications, and/or journalism Or equivalent combination of education and related work experience WORK SCHEDULE: Full-time; Exempt position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM, however, evenings and weekends may be required when needed. PAY STRUCTURE: Grade 6 ($50,461 to $66,851 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Book-Arts-Program-Director_JR101250": {
      "title": "Book Arts Program Director",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : The Book Arts Program Director works in partnership with faculty and library colleagues to incorporate creative expressions of research and to expand modes of book arts research through design thinking, reference, instruction, and outreach services to the campus community, with responsibility for associated curricular and research support for Book Arts study. Works in partnership with faculty and staff as an intellectual partner on book arts project envisioning and implementation across diverse interdisciplinary audiences. Oversees the creation and promotion of studio projects to campus and community members. Assists in stewarding the development of the Book Arts Collection and related materials to support research and instruction needs of students, faculty, independent researchers, and community members. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Research and Instruction: Providing collaborative support for faculty wishing to incorporate book arts content, methods, tools, and technologies into their teaching methodology to facilitate book arts project envisioning and definition, and advise about project scope, requirements, specifications, and/or design. Creates instructional resources, tutorials, provide instruction, and offer research and consultation appointments to students, faculty, and other members of the campus and community as required, using pedagogical best practices and current instructional technologies. Promotes and provides individual book arts scholarship research consultations Assists with collection development for Book Arts technical resources, book arts collection materials, and artists books. Collaborates with colleagues in the library (and elsewhere) to coordinate services and programming utilizing the Book Arts Studio. Develops Book Arts Studio curriculum, workshops, and programs as a university-wide and community resource. Conducts environmental scans of University of Richmond classes to identify current and emerging scholarly projects, areas of inquiry, and pedagogical trends. Aligns library resources as appropriate to support these departments. Develops and provides book arts, history of the book, and letterpress printing information sessions, classroom presentations, training sessions and online services to faculty, students, and community groups; instructs faculty and students on book arts practices, including bookbinding, printing, and studio tool usage. Assesses student learning outcomes in Book Arts Studio programming, adjusting instruction as necessary. Assists with public service and outreach efforts of the libraries, including exhibits, tours, presentations, and other projects as requested. Book Arts Studio Management : Provides leadership for the Book Arts Studio and oversees all studio activities including planning and programming, supervision of Book Arts Studio Manager, and coordinating equipment maintenance Manages the Book Arts Studio budget, ensuring effective execution, reporting, and planning for the studio’s annual operating budget, including use of restricted and unrestricted funds Identifies Book Arts Studio vendors and coordinates supply ordering process. Collaborates with Head of Book Arts, Archives, and Rare Books on donor relations, including acceptance of donated items, transport to the studio, and studio implementation. Researches new equipment and materials, and trains staff and students on new equipment when the program adds these to the studio. Prototypes demonstration projects and/or implements tools for use by others. Collaborates with the Book Arts Studio Manager to oversee programming, equipment maintenance, open hours, and training/supervision of student employees Professional Development: Participates in professional development and research/creative activities. Serves on Library and/or University committees. Keeps abreast of the current literature on emerging technologies as they relate to book arts production methods, library service, reference service, collection development, outreach and instruction service, and other related areas. QUALIFICATIONS: Thorough knowledge of and demonstrated skills in at least one of the following: hand bookbinding, letterpress printing, artist’s books, and/or the history of the book. Demonstrated knowledge of or experience with emerging trends in bookmaking, letterpress, and book arts. Significant experience teaching and instruction, particularly at the undergraduate level. Energetic teaching and presentation skill, as well as a dedication to student and faculty engagement. Demonstrated interest in professional development that will enhance the candidate’s value to the Libraries, the University, the profession, and the scholarly community. A basic knowledge of library organization, principles, and procedures. Demonstrated ability to communicate clearly, both orally and in writing, about complex, technical processes to non-technical personnel. Demonstrated ability to work in a team environment where consultation, flexibility, collaboration and cooperation are essential. Ability to work and learn independently and to initiate necessary work procedures according to guidelines. Strong organizational and time management skills; ability to work under time constraints and to meet regularly recurring deadlines on multiple, concurrent projects. Evidence of strong analytical problem-solving skills, attention to detail, and results-oriented approaches to complex problems. Superior interpersonal skills and the ability to work collegially with a diverse group of faculty, staff and students on a daily basis. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: MFA degree required, with an emphasis/certificate in book arts, bookbinding, and/or letterpress printing. At least 5 years professional experience in printing, binding, and the book arts. At least 3 years of instructional experience, preferably within an academic environment. At least 2 years of professional experience in management of a studio, bindery, print shop, fine arts, museum, or academic environment. WORK SCHEDULE: Full-time; Exempt Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM SALARY STRUCTURE : Pay Grade 7 ($58,011 to $76,877 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Teaching-Faculty-of-Italian-Studies_JR101411": {
      "title": "Teaching Faculty of Italian Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Languages, Literatures, and Cultures in the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for the position of Teaching Faculty of Italian Studies and Director of the Italian Intensive Language Program , to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. This is a full-time, non-tenure-track, continuing position with benefits. The position includes opportunities for promotion to Senior Teaching Faculty after successful completion of a probationary period and triennial review. We seek a colleague who shares our commitment to student-centered, inclusive undergraduate education. The ideal candidate will also have an interest in supporting student professional development and career readiness. Our department values innovation in teaching, faculty collaboration, cross-disciplinary connections, and inclusive pedagogical practices that respond creatively to the rich diversity of our students’ backgrounds, interests, goals, and experiences. Please follow the link for more information on the Department of Languages, Literatures, and Cultures and its programs. The successful candidate will teach the equivalent of seven classes per academic year, primarily in the intensive language sequence, with opportunities to teach one upper-level course per academic year. Directorship responsibilities will include coordinating language and drill instructors, administering the language curriculum for the beginning and intermediate levels, developing language materials and teaching methods, and overseeing the Italian Club and other extracurricular activities for majors and minors. There will also be opportunities to mentor undergraduate research. We welcome applicants with experience teaching in a variety of educational settings and who demonstrate a commitment to continuous growth as educators. Applicants must hold an MA or Ph.D. in Italian, Romance Studies, Comparative Literature, or a related field with a primary focus on Italian before the start date. Demonstrated advanced proficiency in Italian and English and the ability to teach effectively at all levels of the undergraduate Italian language curriculum are required.  The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $77,000. Applicants should apply online at http://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae , cover letter, and a teaching statement of no longer than two pages. The teaching statement should articulate the candidate’s teaching philosophy, interests, and future development goals, as well as the candidate's involvement in and commitment to educating a diverse student body through inclusive pedagogies. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence on November 15 and continue until the position is filled.  Questions about the application or materials may be directed to the Search Co-Chairs, Lidia Radi and Anthony Russell. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Events-Scheduler_JR101407-1": {
      "title": "Events Scheduler",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: The Events Scheduler is responsible for event scheduling for comprehensive and routine events on campus. Utilizes event scheduling software to reserve campus space and resources for on-campus clients. As a part of the Events team, the scheduler provides support administratively, logistically, and operationally to the events staff, as required. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:   Event Scheduling: (70%) •Processes reservation requests for the use of university space, resources, and equipment. • Analyzes relevant logistical criteria to resolve conflicting space and resource requests. Communicates to all departments involved. • Educates clients about university policies and requirements. • Provides direction to clients for planning and identifying resources for high-quality events and functions, including but not limited to catering, multimedia, and parking. • Monitors food service/alcohol exemptions in the reservation system, requests for services and updates reservations as needed with outside vendor information. • Serves as a liaison with other departments on basic administrative and/or operational matters, as necessary. Administrative Support: (30%) • Performs a variety of office duties to support the smooth and efficient operation of the Events Office, including monitoring email inboxes and paperwork. • Assists in general office duties as needed or assigned; greets and directs visitors; resolves routine administrative problems and answers inquiries regarding activities and operations; accepts, screens, and routes telephone calls; maintains a log of inquiries. • Assists the Events team as assigned including, but not limited to, events such as commencement, orientation, and family weekends. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Work is normally performed in a typical office environment, with long periods of sitting and working at a computer. Must be able to lift 15 pounds. Work requires occasional nights and some weekends, including commencement and special university events. QUALIFICATIONS: • Ability to deliver excellent customer service, performing efficiently and courteously in a fast-paced environment. •Ability to adapt quickly and accurately to changing situations. •Ability to work cooperatively as part of a team. •Ability to maintain confidentiality. •Skilled in working with diverse groups of people, handling all interpersonal situations with tact and diplomacy. •Skilled in problem-solving and public relations. •Excellent organizational skills; strong verbal and written communication skills. •General knowledge of event planning procedures and processes. •General knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite and event scheduling software; ability to learn new software. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: • High school diploma or GED required • 2+ years’ experience in direct customer service or an office setting required   •Experience in the events industry preferred • Experience in higher education environment preferred WORK SCHEDULE: Part-time; non-exempt position 9-month position Schedule will vary SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 4 ($18.34 to $24.30 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Post-Baccalaureate-Research-Assistant_JR101329": {
      "title": "Post Baccalaureate Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description ***For best consideration candidates must complete the online application and attach a resume and cover letter*** SUMMARY: The Yang Lab at the University of Richmond (Richmond, VA) is seeking a full-time post-baccalaureate research assistant for a two-year NSF-funded position, start date as soon as possible following completion of the search (anticipated Fall 2026). The lab uses computational methods to study evolutionary genomics, with a focus on human genomic prehistory. The research assistant will develop and apply bioinformatic pipelines for studying genetic adaptation in modern humans, with opportunities for co-authorship on publications. The position is designed for an individual planning to apply to PhD programs who wants substantial independent research beforehand. This position offers $21.00 per hour with full-time benefits including health insurance. RESPONSIBILITIES: Apply, develop, and improve computational pipelines for studying evolutionary genomics in high-performance computing environments. Develop, test, and edit protocols and websites used for Yang Lab associated research and teaching. Perform general tasks that will advance Yang Lab-related research and outreach, e.g. mentoring undergraduate students, organizing summer workshops. Keep accurate and detailed records of analyses and code using a lab notebook and version-controlled workflows. QUALIFICATIONS: Required: Bachelor’s degree in biology or a related field. Previous experience with computing, e.g. experience with Python and/or a Linux environment. Effective written communication skills as evidenced by application materials. Preferred: Demonstrated interest in human evolutionary genomics and/or computational genomics. Biological research experience ideally with computational elements, including troubleshooting and optimizing protocols. Evidence of strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and independent work habits. Strong ability to work in group situations, and to mentor others, particularly undergraduate students. A complete application includes the following: (1) A cover letter outlining relevant experience and interest in the position with specific examples addressing the required and preferred qualifications, (2) a CV/resume highlighting pertinent experience relative to the required and preferred qualifications, and (3) contact information for three professional references. Finalists will be asked to provide an unofficial transcript. Informal queries about the position are welcome and can be directed to Dr. Melinda A. Yang ( myang@richmond.edu ). Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis; early submission encouraged. Apply at   http://jobs.richmond.edu . WORK HOURS: Full-time, non-exempt position This position is a two year limited term role due to expire in 2028 Monday – Friday, 8:30 a.m. – 5:00 p.m.; 7.75 hrs./day, 38.75 hrs./week SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 3 - This position offers $21.00 per hour"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Data-Science-and-Statistics_JR101409": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Data Science and Statistics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Data Science and Statistics Program in the School of Arts & Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. The Data Science and Statistics Program is an interdisciplinary program rooted in the University's liberal arts mission with affiliated faculty from departments across the School of Arts & Sciences. The program offers a minor in data science and statistics and teaches a core sequence of courses in statistical modeling, data science, probability, and statistical learning. The appointment will be housed in the Department of Mathematics and Statistics, with all teaching responsibilities within the interdisciplinary Data Science and Statistics program. The successful applicant is expected to commit to highly effective teaching at all levels of the undergraduate curriculum and to maintain a vigorous research program. Candidates from all subfields of data science and statistics will be considered.  Rather than seeking a particular area of specialization, we are looking for candidates who can teach statistical and computational methods to students from a wide range of majors and who are eager to collaborate with the program's affiliated faculty across the natural sciences, social sciences, and humanities.   A Ph.D. in statistics, data science, computer science, information science, mathematics, or a related field of research is required by the time of appointment.   Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae, a cover letter, a statement describing their teaching experience and interests, and a statement describing their research agenda (suggested length: 2 single-spaced pages per statement). The teaching statement should detail their teaching philosophy and commitment to educating a diverse student body through inclusive pedagogies. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence October 12, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact the search chair, Prof. Taylor Arnold (tarnold2@richmond.edu), with any questions. For more information on the program, please visit https://datascience.richmond.edu.   The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $95,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Asst-Director-Career-Services_JR101362": {
      "title": "Asst Director Career Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: The Assistant Director of Career Services supports the career development and professional advancement of adult learners, graduate and undergraduate students, and alumni through individualized coaching, career education, and alumni engagement. This role designs and delivers career programming tailored to professionals at different stages of their careers, including career changers, working adults, graduate students, and individuals pursuing career advancement or encore careers. The Assistant Director serves as a strategic partner to academic programs across the School of Professional and Continuing Studies, MBA and graduate business programs, Athletics and other adult-focused initiatives. Through career advising, employer and alumni outreach, and collaborative campus partnerships, this position helps students and alumni translate their academic experiences into meaningful professional opportunities. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Minimal to no physical demands. QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, Skills & Ability: Knowledge of career development and student development theories, research and practices, and recruiting trends.  Prefer knowledge of relationship building with a variety of constituents, including but not limited to employer recruiters, alumni development prospects, parents, faculty & staff. Other skills and abilities that are integral to this position are as follows: Ability to advise students, alumni and organizations Extensive career management experience, including working with adult learners and those in career transition Excellent written and verbal communication Extensive ability to collaborate and develop relationships with a wide range of constituents Supervision and leadership skills Outcomes assessment, learning outcomes and evaluation strategies Outstanding teamwork abilities Innovation and creativity in program development, processes, relationship development and other functions of the position Development of metrics and methods for continuous improvement of processes, goals, and strategies Proven presentation, group facilitation, and teaching skills MBTI certification preferred EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Master’s degree in education, counseling, business, higher education administration, human resources, or related field. At least two years of professional experience in career development, graduate school advising, workforce development, human resources, or the equivalent. Skilled presenter with excellent writing and editing skills. Demonstrated strength in fostering inclusion and belonging. Must combine rich understanding of liberal arts with special appreciation for experienced professionals, adult learners, and graduate students WORK HOURS: Full Time, Exempt 7.75 hours/daily, 38.75 hours/weekly SALARY: Pay Grade 6 - Exempt ($50,461.00 - $70,000.00)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Asst-Director-Development--Communications_JR101252": {
      "title": "Asst Director Development  Communications",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description REQUIRED:  Cover letter and Resume POSITION SUMMARY:  The primary role is to manage the overall strategic communications strategy, organize and manage stewardship, and assist in signature events and operations for the Spider Athletic Fund. Reporting to the Assistant Athletic Director of Annual Giving, the Assistant Director will collaborate and participate in successful solicitation activities by creating strategies to increase donor participation rates and donor giving. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Lead all Spider Athletic Fund Strategic Communications (60%) Be creative to tell the story of the Spider student-athlete experience and Spider Athletics Partner with Athletic Public Relations to ensure branding on all items related to external communications Contribute to the creation and implementation of the Spider Athletic Fund’s solicitation and stewardship strategies, including direct mail, email, social media, and other related items. Produce creative content Organize and manage stewardship for the Spider Athletic Fund (20%) Assist in managing and executing the plan for the Spider Athletic Fund stewardship chart, including the organization of all signature donor recognition events. Lead the Spider Athletic Fund’s acknowledgment process. Signature Events and Operations (20%) Lead all communications related to signature events Assist in the planning and preparation of event management and game operation efforts Partner with University Advancement, specifically the Office of Annual Giving and Donor Relations, to coordinate all communications and rapport building. CONTACTS: Advancement Staff – for fundraising strategy and management Spider Athletics Staff – for effective partnership between fundraising and donor relations activities and athletics operations Alumni, parents, friends – for cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship   SUPERVISION EXERCISED: None WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Required to work some evenings and weekends in conjunction with Advancement, Athletics, or other University events. QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability : Excellent interpersonal and leadership skills, along with the ability to work with diverse internal and external constituencies Strong organizational skills and attention to detail, in conjunction with a comfort level of working in a fast-paced environment while handling multiple projects Strong written and oral communication skills Proven ability to work effectively in a collaborative, team environment Experience using Photoshop, InDesign, or other creative development software. Skill in use of personal computers, standard office software applications, and fundraising software Ability to maintain confidentiality Valid driver’s license with a good 3-year driving history Education & experience : Bachelor’s degree required Experience in fundraising, marketing, or other related field preferred. Scheduled Hours :  Mon-Fri, 8:30 a.m. – 5:00 p.m.; 38.75 hours per week; some evenings, weekends and travel required Salary Grade Level 5:  $43,867 to $58,136"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Videographer_JR101349": {
      "title": "Videographer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description A portfolio is required to be considered for this role. Please attach with resume. Job Summary: The Videographer is responsible for assisting in and developing compelling videos to support University Communications marketing strategies and propel the University of Richmond brand across channels including YouTube, Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, and others. The Videographer will assist with professional filming, editing, and providing a finished product. The position collaborates with others on scripts and concepts. Support in the creation of university-level brand storytelling, with audio and video content, by helping take concepts from script to completion. This will include but is not limited to filming, recording audio, lighting set up, teleprompting, audio and video editing, color grading, audio design, and motion graphics. Job Duties: Capture high-quality video footage and audio files using professional-grade equipment Stay current with industry trends and best practices to continually improve video production quality Manage and maintain video equipment, including cameras, lighting, and sound equipment Update/edit video content in response to feedback from University Communications colleagues and campus partners as requested Support senior videographer and video editor as needed Manage, organize, and maintain production files and video libraries Work within University brand guidelines to produce videos for the University’s communication channels utilizing: Effective storytelling techniques Cuts, splices, transitions, and titles Sound effects and audio track Motion graphic animation Color grading and enhancement Creative collaboration Working conditions/physical efforts: Standing or sitting for prolonged periods Walking Lifting and carrying – up to 50 pounds Working hours outside of regular office hours, including evenings and weekends Qualifications: Knowledge, skills & ability: Video camera usage Audio setup and recording Lighting set up Video editing software, including Adobe Premiere, After Effects, Audition, Media Encoder, DaVinci Resolve, and Avid Media Composer Animation and graphic software including Adobe After Effects and the Adobe Creative Suite Color grading, audio mixing, and video output format knowledge A keen eye, attention to detail, and artistic ability Ability to work from storyboards Ability to manage multiple projects from concept to final delivery by meeting deadlines Exceptional communication, interpersonal, and teamwork skills that allow collaboration across teams Excellent time management skills Critical thinking and creative problem-solving talents Education & Experience: A bachelor's degree in film production, media studies, communications, or equivalent A minimum of one year of proven work experience as a Videographer, portfolio required Work Schedule: Full-time; Exempt position Monday-Friday 8:30-5:00PM. This position will require you to work outside of regular office hours to include evenings and weekends. Pay Structure: Grade 5 ($43,867 to $58,136 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Physics_JR101387": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Physics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Physics in the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. We seek a candidate whose research will broaden our department in any of several areas. As a result, applications are encouraged from candidates in all subfields of physics and applied physics, both theoretical and experimental. The successful candidate is expected to have demonstrated a keen interest and ability in undergraduate teaching. The successful candidate will maintain a vigorous research program that engages undergraduates in substantive research outcomes. Courses taught will include workshop-style introductory physics for science majors, upper-division courses for undergraduate physics majors, and possibly general-education courses for non-science majors. Candidates should have a PhD in Physics or a closely related field, and postdoctoral or other research experience beyond the PhD. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, a statement of teaching interests and philosophy (approx. 2-5 single-spaced pages), evidence of teaching effectiveness if available (e.g., sample assignments and other course materials, examples of graded student work, peer and student evaluations, etc.), and a description of a research program, including plans for undergraduate involvement (approx. 2-5 single-spaced pages). The teaching statement should detail the applicant’s teaching philosophy and approaches to inclusive pedagogy and mentoring in support of a diverse undergraduate community. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence on October 1 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Dr. Ted Bunn (ebunn@richmond.edu) with any questions. For more information on the physics department and its programs, please visit https://physics.richmond.edu . The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $85,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. Startup funds will be available. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/HVAC-Mechanic-II_JR100563": {
      "title": "HVAC Mechanic II",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: Provide service and maintenance for all aspects of mechanical operations and heating, ventilating, and air-conditioning (HVAC) and refrigeration equipment (especially large commercial and specialty systems) so as to support the University's educational mission. QUALIFICATIONS: Strong verbal communication and listening skills Maintains a high degree of professionalism Highly thorough and dependable Working knowledge and ability to use the International Mechanical Code and perform work in accordance with IMC Understand basic chemistry, electrical basics and control systems, steam and water heating fundamentals, and HVAC & refrigeration basic cycles and principles Welding and/or soldering skills; and ability to use tools, meters, gauges and equipment correctly and safely Ability to read, understand, and troubleshoot schematic diagrams, and troubleshoot and repair problems of varied types Knowledge of procedures for safe refrigerant and chemical handling Working knowledge of OSHA regulations pertaining to work performed Working knowledge of HVAC and associated systems employing computer-based monitoring and control devices Refrigerant Handling Certification (Universal preferred) upon hire Journeyman Mechanical License upon hire Must have a valid Virginia driver’s license and meets the standards for the University’s insurance carrier upon hire EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE :  H.S. Diploma or GED required Technical training in mechanical work evidenced by obtaining or ability to obtain Journeyman's Mechanical License (typically 3 years of Technical Trade School) required 4-7 years’ progressively responsible mechanical experience directly related to HVAC systems to include commercial HVAC service of chillers and refrigeration required WORK HOURS: Full-time, non-exempt position Monday – Friday, 7:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.; 8 hours a day; 40 hours per week; flexibility required. All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are also subject to planned overtime work as required by the immediate supervisor. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 6 (Hiring Range $24.26 to $36.08 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Compliance-Analyst_JR101388": {
      "title": "Compliance Analyst",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description A cover letter is REQUIRED to be considered for this role. POSITION SUMMARY: The Compliance Analyst supports the University’s compliance and discrimination prevention/Title IX programs to help ensure adherence to applicable federal and state laws, regulations, and institutional policies. This position also assists with compliance education and training initiatives to promote awareness and alignment across the campus community. QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to interpret policies, procedures, and regulations, and analyze information to determine appropriate next steps. Ability to create an inclusive learning environment for all learning needs. Knowledge of learning and organizational development methodologies. Evidence of excellent judgment, complex problem-solving capabilities, and responsiveness in crises and emergent situations. Excellent written and verbal communications skills. Excellent analytical and project management skills. Ability to use tact and discretion, to maintain confidentiality, to interact diplomatically, and to maintain neutrality when working with a broad range of constituents. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a team environment. Proficient in Microsoft Office suite, including advanced Excel functions. Ability to work nights and weekends, as needed. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Bachelor’s degree in a related or applicable area. Two years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience facilitating training programs. Work experience in higher education preferred. WORK SCHEDULE: Full-time; Exempt; 10-month position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM, however, some nights and weekends may be required. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 7 ($58,011 to $76,877)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Manager--Print-Services_JR101365": {
      "title": "Manager, Print Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-05\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: The Manager, Print Services is responsible for all aspects of the University’s print shop, from retail and financial management to physical and online facilities. The Manager sets the vision, exercises leadership, management, and financial skills to provide innovative services, in-store and online, to ensure exemplary customer service, and sound financial practices. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Operations (40%) Manages equipment leases, maintenance, and inventory, applicable point-of-sale systems, vendor relationships, and safety audits. Provides day-to-day operational management of the campus print shop and any satellite operations, including related delivery services. Maintains and enforces established health, safety, and service standards, including University policies and procedures. Works collaboratively with campus constituents to determine service needs. Creates and/or maintains all required logs, records, files, and reports. Develops working knowledge of all duties; provides position relief, as needed.   Outreach and Communications (20%) Improves outreach on- and off-campus to increase sales. Establishes effective partnerships and maintains positive communications and working relationships to ensure effective human relations management and customer satisfaction. Research product trends and develops innovative offerings. Leads in development of marketing materials and promotional events. Projects a positive image to promote print services. Provides diverse revenue streams.   Supervision (20%) Provides overall vision, leadership, and management for print services, supervising a safe, efficient operation and cultivating teamwork and customer service. Sets employee performance expectations, communicates specific instructions with appropriate follow-through. Establishes and maintains policies, procedures, and standards, including but not limited to quality, service, safety, and cleanliness. Oversees scheduling, payroll, and disciplinary procedures. Manages staff; ensures training programs for all staff. Provides coaching and guidance. Encourages professional development opportunities for staff. Financial Administration (20%) Manages billing following established procedures. Helps develop and manage annual budget and monthly income projections. Monitors and forecasts budgets. Adheres to financial goals by controlling expenses. Produces and reviews monthly reports, including profit and loss statements, financial analyses, and benchmarking data. Maintains responsibility for all financial assets of the assigned unit, including equipment, receivables, and all physical inventory, and petty cash. Maintains accounts payable, accounts receivable, and general ledgers, closing books monthly.   SUPERVISION EXERCISED:   Supervises, hires, trains, schedules, and provides work direction and problem-solving assistance for full- and/or part-time employees. Conducts performance reviews, recommends salary increases, promotions, and terminations. Staff: 1-10; students: 1-10 WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: This position requires long periods of standing and walking, and the ability to lift 50 lbs. Must be able to safely use and operate typical printing tools and equipment. Must be able to work outdoors in varying weather conditions, on occasion. Designated non-essential personnel. QUALIFICATIONS: Customer service Communications skills Flexibility skills Organizational/detail oriented Supervisory/leadership skills Analytical skills Math skills Technical skills Computer skills Sales or Account Management General knowledge of printing and mail practices General knowledge of accounting practices Comprehensive knowledge of industry guidelines and legalities Must possess a current Virginia driver’s license with a driving record in good standing EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Bachelor’s degree Minimum of three years’ supervisory experience Five years’ experience in managing a print center; college or university environment preferred Minimum of three years’ experience in sales or account management Combination of education and experience may be considered. WORK SCHEDULE: Full-time; Exempt position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 7 ($58,011 to $86,299 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Sr-Director--Accessibility-Services_JR101342-2": {
      "title": "Sr Director, Accessibility Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description REQUIRED: Resume and Cover Letter The Sr Director of Accessibility Services ’ responsibilities include providing leadership and supervision, ensuring the delivery of services and resources, and overseeing education and outreach. The Sr Director will develop and articulate a shared vision for accessibility, involving students, staff, and faculty. The Sr Director is responsible for planning and directing services for students with accessibility needs at all five (5) UR schools, as well as supporting the accessibility and accommodation needs of UR employees. The Sr Director provides ongoing, collaborative education and outreach regarding accessibility services for members of the UR community, prospective students, parents and other constituencies. As Sr Director, they supervise staff members, allocate resources, and oversee programmatic initiatives. This role also serves as a member of the Health and Well-being Leadership Team. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  LEADERSHIP and SUPERVISION (50%): The Sr Director will develop and articulate a shared vision for campus accessibility, involving students, staff, and faculty. The Sr Director supervises staff members, allocates resources, and oversees programmatic initiatives. Develops annual objectives and long-term goals for student accessibility that align with Health and Well-being, the Division of Student Development, and Human Resources goals Develops and utilizes various short- and long-term assessments to determine the needs of students and employees with disabilities and accessibility needs, and identifies and implements strategies to address those needs Allocates and monitors budget and staff resources Articulates the vision of student accessibility to all members of the UR community; serves as a subject matter expert and the point of contact for various campus committees regarding accessibility Supervises, evaluates and professionally develops staff members, keeping them current with best practices and legal issues impacting student accessibility Evaluates and updates, as needed, accessibility policies and procedures and guidelines for verification of disability/need for students and employees requesting accommodations and services SERVICES and RESOURCES (30%): The Sr Director is responsible for planning and directing services for students with accessibility and accommodation needs at all five (5) UR schools, as well as helping support those needs for UR employees. Reviews disability documentation, evaluates requests for accommodation, advocates for students when needed, and provides reasonable and appropriate accommodations based on current laws, regulations, and best practices Consults with HR partners in review of employee requests and accommodations Serves as the University’s ADA Coordinator Works collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students in five (5) schools to ensure that approved accommodations are provided effectively and efficiently Processes student intakes and maintains an ongoing caseload Generates creative and practical solutions to address current and emerging needs, including accommodating students in online learning environments Identifies, promotes, and oversees opportunities for students and employees with disabilities and accessibility needs to fully participate in the University of Richmond experience Provides input to Facilities Management, Residence Life and Housing, and other departments in the development of a campus-wide accessibility plan, including the physical landscape. Supports the Director in grievance and appeal-related processes involving student accommodations Partners with Human Resources to resolve complex employment situations regarding accommodations and other ADA requirements EDUCATION & OUTREACH (20%): The Sr Director provides ongoing, collaborative education and outreach regarding accessibility services for members of the UR community, prospective students, parents and other constituencies. Develops educational/informational materials and presentations for faculty, staff and students to promote consideration, active inclusion, and removal of barriers for people with disabilities and those in need of accommodations. Consults with the Faculty Hub and the Weinstein Learning Center (WLC) on Universal Design (UD) principles Reviews and recommends updates to the student accessibility website in consultation with the HWB operations team Serves as a collaborative expert by working with General Counsel and educating faculty and staff on appropriate accommodations in compliance with University policy and equal access laws Collaborates with the Division of Inclusion and Belonging to improve the campus climate around disability and access Serves on Division- and University-wide advisory committees and working groups related to accessibility Actively engages in meetings and programs with Deans Offices, colleagues, division-wide activities, staff development programs, and professional development opportunities Other duties as assigned CONTACTS :   All aspects of the University community including students, staff, faculty, and parents/families Regular direct contact with University Counsel’s Office, HR partners and faculty/staff, and the Director of Inclusion and Belonging (in the Division of Inclusion and Belonging) External contact with prospective parents/families and external partners providing accommodation services SUPERVISION EXERCISED : provides direct supervision of at least 3 professional and part-time staff and indirect supervision of student workers WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Requires employee to work mainly inside in an office environment and travel to other locations on campus for meetings and events QUALIFICATIONS : Required knowledge, skills & ability :   Knowledge and understanding of the application of Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act and the subsequent ADAAA, and court decisions and agency rulings as they relate to the higher education context Ability to interpret a wide variety of disability diagnostic assessments and determine accommodations Proven experience developing and implementing policies, procedures, and best practices related to accessibility and inclusion Ability to complete multiple tasks with competing priorities; manage and organize work to consistently meet deadlines Working knowledge of assistive technology and adaptive equipment Ability to develop and maintain partnerships among multiple stakeholders, both on and off campus Preferred qualifications : Experience as a director or other leadership role in a college/university disability/accessibility  services office Experience supervising professional staff Experience working with administration/management at all levels Knowledge of Universal Design (UD) learning styles, strategies, and techniques Required education & experience :  An earned advanced or terminal degree in education, special education, psychology, social work, law, or a related area Minimum of seven (7) years of experience working in a disability-related field at the postsecondary level Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, academic leadership, HR partners, legal counsel, and student-facing offices Scheduled Hours : Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; 38.75 hours per week Salary Range Grade 9, exempt : $77,854 to $121,659"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Public-Relations-Specialist_JR101351": {
      "title": "Public Relations Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Position Summary: The Public Relations Specialist supports the University’s media relations and strategic communications efforts to advance institutional reputation, elevate academic visibility, and promote faculty and staff expertise. This position will Manage and/or support media outreach initiatives, contribute to strategic communications efforts, oversee select digital communication assets, and tack performance metrics related to media engagement and communications impact. Reporting to the Senior Director, Media Relations & Strategic Communications and working in close collaboration with the Media Relations Manager, the specialist helps secure media opportunities and develop compelling storytelling opportunities that strengthen the University’s public profile and support institutional priorities. Job Duties: Media Relations (65%) Develop, write, edit, and distribute media releases, pitches, advisories, and related communications materials to support University initiatives, research, faculty expertise, and institutional priorities. Cultivate and maintain positive working relationships with national, trade, regional, and local media contacts. Support proactive media pitching efforts that increase awareness of university programs, achievements, research, and subject matter experts. Monitor, track, and report key media relations and communications metrics, including media placements, engagement, reach, and other performance indicators. Manage and/or support digital communications assets, including newsroom content, media resources, online press materials, editorial calendar, and related platforms. Strategic Communications Support (35%) Assist with strategic communications planning and execution for institutional announcements, initiatives, and events. Provide communications support during crisis, emergencies, and sensitive messaging situations, as needed, including social listening. Collaborate with colleagues across the communications division, as well as academic and administrative units to identify and promote compelling stories and media opportunities. Support various internal communications efforts, as needed. Qualifications: Knowledge, skills & ability Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Experience writing press releases, media pitches, and digital content. Familiarity with crisis and strategic communications principles and practices. Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Familiarity with media monitoring and analytics tools. Ability to exercise sound judgment and maintain professionalism in sensitive or high-pressure situations. Knowledge of AP Style, Adobe Creative Cloud, and best practices in media relations. Education & experience: Bachelor’s degree in public relations, communications, marketing, or a related field. 3+ years of professional experience in public relations, media relations, or strategic communications. Preferred: Experience in higher education or nonprofit communications. Work Schedule: Full-time; Exempt position 8:30AM-5:00PM, however, this position is subject to occasional on-call status and emergency response. Pay Structure: Grade 6 ($50,461 to $66,851 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Asst-Director-Student-Engagement-and-Bonner-Scholars-Program_JR101320-2": {
      "title": "Asst Director Student Engagement and Bonner Scholars Program",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-31\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Cover letters are REQUIRED to be considered for this position. Applications submitted by August 21st will receive priority review. Position Summary: Develops and implements high-impact civic engagement learning experiences for the Bonner Scholars Program (BSP) and a range of integrated, innovative, civic learning initiatives through the Bonner Center for Civic Engagement (CCE). Includes the creation and delivery of educational content, conducting comprehensive student advising, developing and implementing program recruitment strategies, and supporting student initiatives that catalyze civic reflection and agency, campus and community partnerships, and democratically engaged assessment practices. Bonner Scholars Program Administration & Delivery (70%) Student Programming Creates, implements, and delivers innovative, high-impact educational programming and civic engagement curricula for students participating in the Bonner Scholars Program, supporting their learning, growth, and development. Involves student leadership development, financial stewardship, resource management, and programmatic alignment with the CCE’s learning outcomes. Programming includes, but is not limited to, issue-based and professional development curricula to advance civic action and learning, short-term trips and multi-day educational programs, robust civic action and learning opportunities, and internships that align with community-identified goals and needs in Richmond, VA, and beyond.  Designs, develops, and delivers needed resources and opportunities that facilitate meaningful onboarding, engagement, and reflection for students from diverse backgrounds who are committed to civic engagement. Utilizes inclusive facilitation and pedagogical strategies to create collaborative learning environments that incorporate diverse perspectives and foster student agency and leadership. Provides needed input into the CCE’s approach to developing, implementing, and sustaining strategic off-campus relationships in response to campus and community-identified needs. Liaises with Bonner Foundation staff to share educational resources, participate in learning communities, and strengthen network collaboration.  Student Leadership Development & Advising Supports the recruitment and supervision of the Bonner Scholar student leadership team, consisting of students who take lead roles in educational programming, full participation, and retention of peers in student engagement experiences. Includes hiring, training, goal development, and resolving work-related challenges. Supports students in the development of their agency and program leadership through student-led program creation and implementation. Includes cohort meetings, community-building experiences, and annual overnight cornerstones. Initiates and provides high-quality advising by creating resource guides, offering one-to-one guidance, and offering professional development trainings to ensure meaningful and impactful participation in long-term, community-based, civic engagement experiences for students from diverse backgrounds. Provides relevant programmatic support for academic-year service requirements for select cohorts in the Bonner Scholars Program. BSP Admissions  Works with the Bonner Scholars Program Director, University Admissions, and communications staff to deliver and implement strategies to promote the Bonner Scholars Program to incoming students and Walk-on scholars. Supports and implements strategies to evaluate Bonner applicants by coordinating selection committees, rubrics, and comprehensive applicant analysis. Enrolls new Bonner Scholars and works with Academic Advising to ensure successful onboarding. Miscellaneous Provides additional programmatic support to contribute to the success of the program and fulfill Foundation expectations, at the discretion of the Bonner Scholars Program Director. Bonner Center for Civic Engagement (CCE) initiatives and programs (20%) Contributes to and leads select, CCE programs by working with multiple campus stakeholders to establish outcomes, timelines, resource management, content delivery, and evaluation methods. Contributes to high-impact, student-led community-engagement experiences focused on select, community-identified, issue-based needs to advance civic action and learning. May include, but not limited to, weekly community engagement experiences, short-term trips, multi-day educational programs, community-engaged learning workshops, and group dialogues. Develops, and stewards select campus partnerships, supporting short—and long-term goals, strategic planning, and program development. Includes liaising with students, faculty, and staff leadership across campus, identifying opportunities for student connectivity, civic engagement, and leadership. Represents the CCE and the Student Engagement Team on select programmatic committees to uphold a community-engaged perspective and support the implementation of programs that reflect the CCE’s values. Assessment, learning, and professional development (5%) Assessment Delivers and implements high-impact assessment tools and maintains evaluative data designed to enhance student reflection and learning and strengthen the impact of cocurricular civic and community engagement programs. Includes the evaluation of student experiences, updating relevant records; and contributing to special reports for a variety of audiences. Evaluates assessment-related strategies to understand and strengthen the impact of co-curricular civic engagement programs. Learning and Professional Development Contributes to and participates in the learning and development of best practices of civic engagement and diversity, equity and inclusion in a higher ed context through a variety of avenues (e.g., conferences, webinars, and staff trainings). Promotes and represents the CCE in select national, civic-engagement scholarship networks. May promote and represent the CCE and UR in national networks dedicated to advancing the work of civic engagement in higher ed. Additional Responsibilities (5%) Performs miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Knowledge, skills & abilities:  Collaboration and Relationship-building Strong interpersonal communication skills and the ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Ability to build strong relationships with students, faculty, community partners, and staff Communication Excellent writing and oral communication skills Strong Proficiency in Microsoft products, including Word, Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint Ability to create, compose, and edit written materials. Administration Ability to gather data and compile information Record maintenance skills. Database management skills. Demonstrated ability to maintain confidentiality. Strong project management and budgetary skills Ability to quickly learn new web-based tools (e.g., AirTable, MailChimp, Bannerweb, Calendly) Program Management Ability to assess and evaluate programs Skill in organizing resources, establishing priorities, and multi-tasking. Ability to analyze and solve problems. Student development Ability to recruit, train, and motivate volunteers Ability to recruit, train, and supervise student interns  Ability to lead, train, and support student leaders Ability to manage student employees Ability and desire to utilize arts-integrated teaching methods Demonstrated investment in the intersection of community engagement and higher education Demonstrated commitment to learning and critical reflection Knowledge of and experience with curriculum development Knowledge of and experience with civic education and civic skills Civic Engagement Knowledge of and experience with social issues Knowledge of and experience with non-profits Education and Experience: Bachelor’s degree required A minimum of two years’ experience working in higher ed or k-12 is required. Work Schedule: Full-time; Exempt position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM, however, some nights and weekends may be required. Pay Structure: Grade 6 ($50,461 to $66,851 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Legal-Assistant-Office-Manager_JR101373": {
      "title": "Legal Assistant/Office Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-30\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: Serves as the legal assistant for the General Counsel’s office and the and office manager for the division, which includes University Compliance, Risk Management, and the Office of General Counsel. QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to create, compose, and edit written materials with careful attention to detail . Strong interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Ability to investigate and analyze information and draw conclusions . Knowledge of subpoena management and response . Knowledge of legal contracts, agreement and settlement procedures and documentation Ability to draft legal documents . Knowledge of the principles and procedures of legal research . Highly Proficient with Microsoft Office products, Adobe Acrobat, internet search engines, and ability to learn cloud-based contract management system and Workday (University Enterprise Resource Planning system). Demonstrated ability to exercise independent judgment and work independently and under pressure to meet critical deadlines . Demonstrated ability to maintain a high level of professionalism, confidentiality, and efficiency . EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Bachelor’s degree required. Paralegal certificate preferred. Minimum of five years’ experience as a legal assistant in an in-house legal department or law firm, with progressively increasing responsibilities. WORK SCHEDULE: Full-time; Exempt position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 7 ($58,011 to $86,299 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Cook-I_JR101347": {
      "title": "Cook I",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-22\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description JOB SUMMARY: As an entry level kitchen position working under direction, this position prepares food in quantity and is responsible for maintaining high standards for food quality while practicing proper sanitation, food safety, and cooking procedures. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Operations : • Cooks and prepares food as directed according to established recipes, special dietary or nutritional restrictions, or numbers of portions. Cooking may include display cooking • Cleans, cuts, and cooks all food products as assigned, including meat, fish, poultry, starches, and vegetables • Garnishes foods • Cleans, sets up, and breaks down kitchen equipment, appliances, and work areas to ensure cleanliness and functional operation • Operates kitchen, line equipment and machinery according to established safety procedures • Covers, labels, and dates all food products, and follows all food safety rules for proper storage • Monitors serving lines and ensures an adequate supply of food items, per the menu of the day, for the entire service period • Monitors and records temperatures of foods stored in refrigeration, in hot boxes, and on the serving line to ensure safe temperatures • Performs other duties, as assigned Additional Duties : • Maintains cleanliness of the productions areas • Washes pots, pans, dishes, utensils, and other cooking equipment, as needed CONTACTS : Contacts are primarily with direct supervisor and others in group or department to give and receive information. SUPERVISION EXERCISED : None WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT : Food services QUALIFICATIONS : Knowledge, skills & ability : • Ability to simultaneously handle multiple priorities • Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills • Possesses flexibility to work in a fast paced, dynamic environment • Friendly and service-oriented • Demonstrates a high commitment to quality • Excellent collaboration and team building skills Basic knowledge of culinary practices and techniques • Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices • Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire, required. Required dependent upon assigned unit Education & experience : • H.S. Diploma or GED required • One year of related work experience preferred Work Hours Full Time Non-exempt Monday-Friday 6:30am - 3:00pm weekends as needed Salary: Pay Grade 1 ($17.00/hour - $18.50/hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Cafe-Associate_JR101343": {
      "title": "Cafe Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : Entry level position on the café team responsible for assisting in front-and back-of-house responsibilities: prepares and cooks high quality food; staffs any function in the front-of-house, as needed; maintains food quality, sanitation, and safety standards; and provides exemplary customer service. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: This position is designated as essential personnel and, during inclement weather and emergency situations, is required to be available to the University as needed. This position requires long periods of standing and walking, and the ability to lift 50 lbs., in conditions that are often, hot, humid, or cold. Must be able to safely use and operate typical kitchen tools and equipment. Must be able to work outdoors in varying weather conditions, on occasion. Youth age 16 and 17 are not permitted to operate, feed, set-up, adjust, repair or clean hazardous equipment, e.g., chopping or slicing machines, commercial mixers, power driven bakery machines, trash compactor, etc. QUALIFICATIONS : Basic knowledge of culinary techniques and practices Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices Basic knowledge of computers and the ability to learn software Ability to efficiently and accurately operate point-of-sale register systems Ability to use effective time management to accomplish tasks Skilled in reading and written and verbal communication Ability to understand and follow written and verbal instructions and procedures Ability to exercise flexibility and to work calmly and efficiently under pressure in a fast-paced environment with frequent interruptions Ability to provide a high level of customer service Ability to monitor and maintain quality control standards Ability to work cooperatively as a part of a team EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE : High school diploma or GED required or in the process of pursing a high school diploma or GED which must be obtained within 2 years of date of hire Minimum of 1 year cooking/ cold food prep and cashiering experience preferred Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire (This is dependent on the assigned unit.) WORK HOURS : Part Time, Non-exempt Only works August to May Monday-Thursday 10:30am - 7:00pm, Friday 9:00am - 5:30pm SALARY Pay Grade 1 ($16.50/hour to $18.00/hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Custodial-Floor-Technician_JR101328": {
      "title": "Custodial Floor Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : Performs various floor care maintenance functions in accordance with all University policies, procedures and guidelines; exemplifies and upholds the Facilities Department's mission, which is to create and maintain an outstanding learning environment through constant attention to our community's needs and thoughtful care of our beautiful campus. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: QUALIFICATIONS: Highly thorough and dependable Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills Ability to work within tight timeframes and meet strict deadlines Strong verbal communication and listening skills Demonstrated ability to comply to safety standards and perform work using safe work habits Proficient in the use of custodial equipment with knowledge of all aspects of relating to good cleaning practices and chemical and equipment use care EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: H.S. Diploma or GED required 2- 4 years custodial operation or related field preferred Less than 2 years of professional experience in floor care preferred WORK HOURS: Full Time, Non-exempt Thursday - Sunday 9:30pm - 6:00am SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 2 (Hiring Range $16.01 to $19.21 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Psychology_JR101323": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Psychology in the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. We are seeking a faculty colleague with training and expertise in cognitive development who would contribute to the Department of Psychology as well as the Interdisciplinary Program in Cognitive Science. The position requires a 3-2 teaching load and an active research program involving undergraduates. Service expectations are tailored to career trajectory. A completed Ph.D. in Psychology, Cognitive Science, or a closely related field by the time of appointment is required, as is the capacity to (1) effectively teach courses in cognitive development; foundational courses in our department and in the Cognitive Science major; and advanced courses in area(s) of expertise; (2) establish and maintain an active program of research in cognitive development that closely involves undergraduate researchers. We welcome applicants using diverse methodological approaches and interdisciplinary perspectives that enrich the study of cognitive development. In the Department of Psychology, we value inclusive, high-impact teaching and mentored research that support students from varied educational, cultural, linguistic, and socioeconomic backgrounds, including first-generation and neurodiverse students. We also value rigorous scientific inquiry that deepens understanding of behavior and mental processes and addresses pressing civic and social issues while advancing peer-reviewed scholarship. The department supports collaborative undergraduate research through small class sizes, close faculty-student mentoring, and institutional resources that foster student engagement in scholarship. We are a collegial department in which faculty, student, and staff interactions reflect genuine care, concern, and respect for one another, and we strive to promote the professional growth, well-being, and sustainable career development of all members of our community. We are committed to evolving our practices to encompass evidence-based innovations in teaching and research. For example, our curriculum is currently being revised to more comprehensively align with updated APA guidelines while reflecting our department values and aspirations to provide students with outstanding opportunities to develop skills that prepare them to enter graduate programs and/or the workforce. Applicants should apply online at http://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae, a cover letter, and two statements in line with the guidelines described below (suggested length: 2 single-spaced pages per statement), demonstrating evidence of the potential for contributions to teaching and mentoring and to research and scholarship in alignment with our departmental and institutional values. Both statements will be evaluated with equal weight. Evidence of (potential for) teaching effectiveness, if available, should be collated and submitted as a single attachment. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to submit a brief video demonstration of teaching effectiveness and to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Statement #1: Describe your teaching and mentoring philosophies, practices, experiences, future plans, and commitment to educating a diverse student body through inclusive pedagogy. Strong applicants will demonstrate the potential to effectively teach courses in cognitive development; foundational courses in our department (e.g., Introduction to Psychological Science, Methods and Analyses); courses in our Interdisciplinary Program in Cognitive Science (e.g., Cognitive Science); and advanced courses in their areas of expertise. In addition, strong candidates will convey their ability to mentor undergraduates in their research program. Applicants are encouraged to submit evidence of (and/or the potential for) teaching effectiveness (e.g., sample syllabus, graded assessment with feedback, favorable student or peer evaluations of instruction, engagement in professional development activities). Statement #2: Describe your current research program and outcomes, as well as future research plans. Strong applicants will demonstrate potential to produce high-quality, peer-reviewed scholarship and to sustain an independent program of research that makes an impact on the field and/or addresses issues of civic and social concern. Applicants are encouraged to address the feasibility of conducting research in our undergraduate setting. For more information on faculty roles and resources, please visit the University website: https://www.richmond.edu/ . Applicants may find information available via the Faculty Handbook, the Faculty Hub, the Office of the Vice President for Inclusion and Belonging, the Bonner Center for Civic Engagement, and Grants Office particularly helpful. Review of applications will commence on September 8, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Dr. Cindy Bukach, Professor of Cognitive Neuroscience and Co-Chair of the Search Committee, at cbukach@richmond.edu with questions. The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $85,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Data-Analytics_JR101321": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Data Analytics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-15\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Interdisciplinary and Professional Studies in the School of Professional and Continuing Studies at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Data Analytics to begin in the 2026-2027 academic year. This is a twelve-month appointment with benefits. The initial appointment will be made for a probationary period of up to three years, with opportunity for recurring, continuing appointments of up to five years per appointment. The School of Professional and Continuing Studies (SPCS) serves working adult students through flexible undergraduate and graduate programs that combine academic rigor with real-world relevance. We seek a teacher-scholar with expertise in data analytics who is committed to the academic and professional success of adult learners and who will contribute to the continued development of our Data Analytics program. Data Analytics curriculum is embedded in academic programs housed in the Department of Interdisciplinary & Professional Studies, which includes academic programming in data analytics, information systems and cybersecurity, human resource management, liberal arts, nonprofit studies, and organizational studies and effectiveness. The successful candidate will teach five courses per year across both undergraduate and graduate data analytics curricula, including courses in data literacy, visualization, statistical analysis, and related areas. Additional responsibilities include developing and updating curriculum in response to industry trends and student needs, maintaining active scholarly engagement in data analytics that informs teaching and supports curriculum development, collaborating with adjunct faculty to ensure instructional quality and consistency, participating in program assessment and accreditation activities, supporting student recruitment through occasional program representation at events, serving on School and University committees, and carrying out other functions as may be appropriate to the position. SPCS faculty enjoy hybrid teaching formats and generous professional development funding. Ability to teach across data analytics and information systems, particularly in a higher education setting and with experience teaching adult and continuing learners, is strongly preferred. Additional areas of interest or experience that are preferred: industry experience in data analytics or related field; online or hybrid teaching and course development; curriculum development and program assessment; and ability to contribute to other SPCS disciplines as program needs evolve. A Ph.D. or DBA in data analytics, data science, information systems, statistics, mathematics, operations research, or a closely related field is required by the time of appointment. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, and teaching statement (maximum two pages). The teaching statement should detail the candidate’s teaching philosophy and commitment to educating a diverse student body through inclusive pedagogies. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence August 10, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Lionel Mew, Assistant Professor of Information Systems and Search Chair, at lmew@richmond.edu with any questions. For more information on SPCS and its programs, please visit https://spcs.richmond.edu. The starting salary for this position is budgeted at $79,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate's individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Mathematics_JR101286": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-13\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics in the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. The department welcomes applicants from a variety of fields within mathematics. We are especially interested in candidates with expertise in applied mathematics, broadly defined, whose scholarship interests can support our thriving mathematical economics major ( https://math.richmond.edu/academics/mathecon/index.html ). A doctoral degree in mathematics, applied mathematics, statistics, or a related field must be completed prior to the start date. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to liberal arts teaching by educating students across the curriculum, contributing to our core mathematics program, and offering upper‑level electives in their area of expertise. The position carries a 3‑2 undergraduate teaching load. We expect candidates to maintain an active research program with the potential to involve undergraduates in meaningful research experiences. Faculty members are expected to serve on department and university committees and are encouraged to participate in campus-wide initiatives supporting our strategic priorities of academic excellence, belonging and community, and experiential learning. New faculty can participate in a year-long collaborative mentoring program and are also supported by programming developed by the Faculty Hub ( https://facultyhub.richmond.edu/ ). The Department of Mathematics and Statistics offers a vibrant program that equips students with theoretical and computational skills for success in many areas of employment, graduate school, and other post-graduation opportunities ( https://math.richmond.edu ). Applicants should apply online at http://jobs.richmond.edu , where they should submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, teaching statement, and research statement. In their cover letter, candidates should address their experience with, or commitment to, fostering an inclusive and welcoming campus community for a diverse student body. The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $85,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. Candidates for this position might be asked at a later date to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Questions about the application or materials may be directed to Search Chair Dr. Joanna Wares, at jwares@richmond.edu. Review of applications will commence on October 5, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Nutrition-Intern_JR101322": {
      "title": "Nutrition Intern",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-09\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: Nutrition Intern is an entry level nutrition position. The nutrition intern will support the campus dietitians by assisting in the demonstration kitchen, nutrition-related projects, and education initiatives.  This internship provides individuals with experience in campus wellness, foodservice operations, and nutrition education.    WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT:   This position is designated as non-essential personnel. This position requires periods of standing, walking, stooping, bending, and reaching, and the ability to lift 50 lbs., in conditions that are often, hot, humid, or cold. Must be able to safely use and operate typical kitchen tools and equipment. QUALIFICATIONS: Currently pursuing or a desire to pursue a degree in Nutrition, Dietetics, Public Health or a related field. Strong interest in food, wellness, and public health in a college campus setting. Skilled in using effective time management to accomplish tasks. Skilled in reading and written and verbal communication. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to understand and follow written and verbal instructions and procedures. Ability to provide a high level of customer service. Ability to work cooperatively as a part of a team. WORK HOURS: Wednesday 1pm - 7pm, Thursday and Friday 9am - 4pm August - May PAY RANGE: Pay Range 1 ($15.00/hr-17.00/hr) EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Currently pursuing or intend to complete a college degree. Safe food handler certification preferred; required within one year of hire."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-of-Accounting_JR101299": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Accounting",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-29\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Accounting in the Robins School of Business at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. The Department holds separate AACSB accreditation. Its mission is to combine superior accounting instruction and intellectual inquiry within a liberal arts environment to prepare individuals for successful careers in accounting and business. The overall standard for the Robins School of Business is excellence in teaching, intellectual contributions in high-quality academic journals, and service. Salary, benefits, and support are competitive with other AACSB accredited schools. Tenure-track faculty members typically teach a 3:2 load. The Robins School is committed to mentoring a diverse and inclusive community of undergraduate students, and small class sizes of  20-25 students allow our faculty to get to know their students very well. A Ph.D. in Accounting is required by the time of appointment. Preference will be given to candidates with interest in teaching in the areas of  Accounting Information Systems (AIS) and/or Audit wi th a commitment to incorporating data analytics and artificial intelligence into course content . Professional work experience related to accounting and auditing and/or a professional certification in Accounting (such as being a licensed CPA) is preferred but not required. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, and confirmation of teaching experience. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence on July 15 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Professor Marshall Geiger or Professor Abdullah Kumas with any questions. [For more information on the Department of Accounting and its programs, please visit https://robins.richmond.edu/undergraduate/departments/accounting/index.html ] The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Event-Police-Officer_JR100784": {
      "title": "Event Police Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-26\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: Temporary Police Officers are currently sworn with the City of Richmond Police Department or Henrico County Division of Police and work at the University of Richmond in an off-duty capacity.  A sworn law enforcement position to provide law enforcement and crime prevention duties to include CLERY requirements for the University of Richmond within the University of Richmond Police Department. RESPONSIBILITIES: Provide law enforcement staffing for campus events  Protects community and property of the University. Performs traffic direction and pedestrian crossing duties as requested Provides safety and security services to the University Community. Promotes a positive community relation in the performance of job duties. Willing to work overtime assignments when needed. The person in this position has been identified as a Campus Security Authority and Responsible Employee for Clery and Title IX purposes respectively, the employee will maintain current training and comply with the responsibilities of both titles. Enforcement  Enforces VA Criminal and Traffic Laws. Makes arrests. Testifies in court. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT Police officers possess a level of fitness and general health to perform physical tasks encountered on the job. These activities may include, but are not limited to: climbing obstacles, running after suspects, running up stairs, jumping over obstacles, lifting heavy objects or persons, subduing persons resisting arrest, and physically pushing movable objects. Officers are physically able to perform the duties and functions of a sworn police officer, carry and use protective equipment, and perform in all weather conditions. QUALIFICATIONS: Able to operate a motor vehicle. Possesses a Virginia Driver’s License and must pass all University driving requirements. Maintains all training standards and firearm requirements established by DCJS and URPD. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Required - Current sworn officer with the City of Richmond Police Department or Henrico County Division of Police. WORK HOURS: Works as needed, temporary, non-exempt position Responds, if requested or needed, on weekends and/or nights as a function of this position  SALARY STRUCTURE: $50.00 per hour"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Modlin-Center-Professional-Technician_JR100428": {
      "title": "Modlin Center Professional Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-25\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description The Modlin Center Professional Technician position is continuously recruited, based on the Modlin Center for Theatre and Arts performance schedule. This position performs technical duties in the areas of audio, lighting, video and projections, carpentry and rigging, and/or other technologies needed to support events at the Modlin Center complex, or in other Richmond area venues as assigned by the Production Manager or Assistant Production Managers. The Modlin Center for the Arts facility houses the Modlin Arts series, Departments of Theatre & Dance, Art & Art History, University Museums, and Music, and is open for use by the campus and local communities. Grade 4 Hourly position: Rate is based on skill level, $18.34 to $24.30 This is an as-needed, part-time, casual position. Work calls will typically be scheduled on a monthly basis, depending on the performance schedule of The Modlin Center."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/SPCS-Non-Credit-Instructor_JR100012": {
      "title": "SPCS Non-Credit Instructor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-25\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description This posting is set up to receive applications for Non-Credit Instructors already identified by University of Richmond’s School of Professional & Continuing Studies. We are not currently recruiting for these roles and you should  only apply if you have been specifically instructed to  by either University of Richmond’s Human Resource Department or the School of Professional & Continuing Studies. If you are interested in other employment opportunities at the University of Richmond please revisit our Career’s site at  http://jobs.richmond.edu . Salary (dependent on program): $20.00 to $100.00 per hour"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Access-Control-Specialist_JR101275": {
      "title": "Access Control Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY : The Access Control Specialist is responsible for mechanical keying systems in addition to installing and servicing electric strikes, magna-locks, motion detectors, card readers ,  power supplies, circuit breakers ,  solenoids, transmitter wall switches, automatic door openers and controls, panic bars, door closers, and digital entry systems. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Direct Responsibilities Responsible for specifying and installing electronic access systems to include card readers, cable installations, power supplies and related electronic hardware. This includes collaborating with end user, One Card office and URPD to ensure compliance, safety and University standards are met. Performs skilled locksmith work by installing and maintaining different types of building locks including cylindrical, mortise, panic hardware, door closers and digital access systems. Assists in maintaining Master Key Systems for University security by keeping accurate records of hardware changes and new lock installations in each building. Provides assistance during renovation of existing or construction of new facilities by determining the requirements for locking devices and systems from blueprints, schematics or sketches, making recommendations for such systems based upon knowledge of available products, and installing or monitoring the installation of locking devices or systems on renovated or newly constructed facilities. Maintains security of lock shop to include key management software and maintains adequate stock of equipment inventory as needed. Assist in maintaining compliance in relation to university policy as it pertains to departmental key audits, departmental record keeping and inspection of lock boxes. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Will be exposed to loud noise (equipment) and product fumes (stains, paints, etc.) in the shop or on work sites. Must be able to lift 50 pounds. Physical capable of working while bending, stooping, kneeling . Capable of working in all types of weather conditions such as rain, flooding, snow, ice, hot and cold temperatures. All Facilities employees are expected to exercise behavior in accordance with the University policies as well as with the Facilities Mission and Statement of Core Values. All Facilities employees are expected to comply with safety standards and demonstrate safe work habits. QUALIFICATIONS: Required ability to install and repair card access and electronic lock systems. Preferred knowledge of various master key system. Must demonstrate confidentiality as necessary. Required knowledge of the safe use and operation of hand and power tools while following safety guidelines. REQUIRED: Must have a valid Driver’s License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing . EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: High school diploma or GED. Minimum of five years’ experience working with access control in an institutional or commercial setting. WORK HOURS: Full-Time; non-exempt position Monday – Friday, 7:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.; 8 hours a day; 40 hours per week; flexibility required. All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are also subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are also subject to planned overtime work as required by the supervisor. PAY STRUCTURE: Grade 5 Hourly ($21.09 to $27.95 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Digital-Communications-Manager---Law-School_JR101274-2": {
      "title": "Digital Communications Manager - Law School",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description A resume and cover letter are required to be considered for this position. Finalists will be asked to provide a professional work sample. POSITION SUMMARY: Reporting to the Director of Communications and Marketing, the Digital Communications Manager will shape the Law School’s digital footprint. The DCM defines and executes the Law School’s communications strategy across all digital platforms, including websites, social media, email, SEO/GEO, and advertising. The DCM will support institutional marketing and communications efforts by creating and curating shareable content to reach a variety of internal and external audiences. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Strategy & Content Development: 40%                 Develop and execute the School’s digital communications strategy across websites, social media, and email to drive key business objectives including brand awareness, reputation management, recruitment, and philanthropic engagement Collaborate with School departments to evaluate and recommend best digital solutions for their goals. Define key metrics and analyze data for university properties, GEO/SEO and search tools, web accessibility, and advertising/campaign asset performance to continually evolve and improve. Monitor and evaluate social and digital trends to identify emerging opportunities to grow key audiences and increase engagement across platforms. Collaborate with colleagues in University Communications to optimally integrate and align the School’s digital strategies with overall institutional strategy. Maintain a pulse on school-wide programming and achievements to proactively identify opportunities for content. Implement best practices in digital communications, marketing, higher education, and legal education to improve digital presence and enhance user experience. Balance multiple projects and deadlines while creating high-quality content.   Digital Platform Management: 30% Oversee content management for the School’s website, social media, and other digital communications channels. Regularly conceptualize, create, and publish content that enhances affinity for the School, communicates successes, builds connections, and encourages engagement. Monitor social media mentions and, in partnership with the Director of Communications & Marketing, determine appropriate responses and address areas of concern. Conduct front-line quality assurance testing for web-based and digital products Develop and maintain editorial calendar for content across digital platforms. Conduct regular platform audits to ensure content is accessible, up-to-date, accurate, and optimized. Visual Content Creation and Art Direction: 20% Serve as the School’s primary photographer and videographer. Organize and direct photo and video shoots, provide photo and video coverage for events, develop scripts, cast projects, manage postproduction. Conceptualize and integrate video and photography into digital platforms. Data Analysis: 10% Develop KPIs to evaluate content performance.  Monitor, analyze, and report on website and social media data and trends to determine successes and recommend areas of opportunity for current and future brand advancement. CONTACTS: Law School Director of Communications & Marketing Law School Associate Director of Communications & Events Multimedia Production & Technology Specialist University Web and Communications Teams SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Supervises the occasional work of freelance vendors and contractors. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Office environment. Computer use is required, as well as light physical effort, such as lifting cameras, video equipment, etc.  QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability : Strong understanding of modern web and digital platforms Strong understanding of modern content strategy definition and execution Strong knowledge of web/digital best practices, including usability, accessibility, and mobile/responsive web Demonstrated social media platform fluency as part of a business strategy Deep knowledge of web content management systems, such as Wordpress, Cascade, or Terminal Four Familiarity with social media management tools (i.e. Hootsuite, Sprout Social) Strong knowledge of website and social media analytics tools (i.e. GA4) Demonstrated understanding of search engine optimization (SEO)/generative engine optimization (GEO) Proficiency in relevant software, including Adobe Creative Suite and Canva Ability to learn new programs and adapt to new technologies quickly Strong understanding of digital marketing principles, best practices in communications, and brand management Exceptionally skilled at project management and multitasking Strong eye for design and digital storytelling Clear and concise written and verbal communication skills Ability to collaborate and an affinity for teamwork Education & experience : Required : Bachelor’s degree with 3 years’ experience in communications, marketing, web design, journalism, or graphic design. Preferred : Experience with writing for a variety of audiences and channels, web content development, social media content development and management, or data analytics are a plus. Preferred : Experience with video editing, email marketing, photography, videography, or graphic design are a plus. Pay Structure : Salaried Exempt position, Grade level 6: $58,656 to $75,046 Schedule Hours: 38.75 hours per week. The typical schedule will be weekdays from 8:30 a.m. to 5 p.m. Occasional evening work will be required."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Cook-II_JR101272": {
      "title": "Cook II",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-23\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : As primary food production staff working under direction, this position exercises some decision making in the preparation of food in quantity for Dining Services customers and is responsible for maintaining high standards for food quality while practicing proper sanitation, food safety, and cooking procedures.   WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Office environment QUALIFICATIONS : Ability to simultaneously handle multiple priorities Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills Possesses flexibility to work in a fast paced, dynamic environment Friendly and service-oriented Demonstrates a high commitment to quality Excellent collaboration and team building skills Intermediate knowledge of culinary techniques and practices Knowledge of food quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices Skilled in high-volume and high-quality food production and presentation Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire required. This is dependent on the assigned unit EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE:  H.S. Diploma or GED required Two years’ experience in high volume production in hotel, restaurant, or college foodservice required WORK HOURS: Full Time, Non-exempt Sunday - Saturday, AM hours (6:00am-3:30pm) rotating weekends SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 2 (Hiring Range $17.75 to $19.50 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Parent-Giving-Officer_JR101232": {
      "title": "Parent Giving Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-22\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description ****For consideration, please attach a resume and cover letter to your application**** SUMMARY : Reporting to the Director of Parent Giving, the Parent Giving Officer is a frontline fundraiser responsible for securing five, six, and seven figure gift commitments to advance the strategic priorities of the University of Richmond. This role manages a portfolio of rated parent prospects with major-gift capacity, cultivates and sustains membership in the President’s Parent Leadership Council (PPLC), and contributes to broader Parent Giving initiatives. This position also plans and manages Welcome to the Web events. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Minimal physical effort required Minimal exposure to physical risk Mon-Fri, 8:30 a.m. – 5:00 p.m. with extended hours as necessary Seasonal, evening, and weekend work will be periodically required throughout the year (e.g. Family Weekend, PPLC programs, Welcome to the Web events, Homecoming, Reunion, etc.) Frequent travel is expected QUALIFICATIONS:          Knowledge, skills & ability :    Demonstrated record of soliciting and closing five and six-figure philanthropic gifts. Ability to exercise good judgment, demonstrate an understanding of the ethical principles associated with working in development, and to use discretion in interactions with members of the University of Richmond community. Excellent interpersonal and leadership skills, along with the ability to work with diverse internal and external constituencies. Proven ability to work effectively in a collaborative, team environment with a high degree of collegiality; honesty and transparency are two other important abilities. Strong written, communication, organizational, and analytical skills as well as a sense of humor. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE :  Bachelor’s degree required Minimum of four to six years of experience in fundraising or equivalent years of experience with a proven track record of success. Higher education fundraising experience is preferred. SALARY STRUCTURE : Salary Range begins at: $77,854 to $121,659 annually, commensurate with experience. Job title will be commensurate with experience (Assistant Director, Associate Director, or Sr. Associate Director -of Parent Giving). This position is based onsite at the University of Richmond. While primarily in-person, there may be opportunities for a hybrid work arrangement depending on departmental needs and individual performance."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Piano-Studies_JR101278-2": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Piano Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Music in the School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Richmond welcomes applications for a full-time tenure-track Coordinator of Piano Studies at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. We seek an active performer with a strong background in Western classical repertoire and piano pedagogy who can successfully balance teaching responsibilities with ongoing professional activity. The successful candidate will be committed to helping students develop a strong foundation in classical piano technique, teaching a studio of approximately ten undergraduate piano students in private lessons each semester, and offering one additional course each academic year related to their area of expertise. As Coordinator of Piano Studies, the successful candidate will work collaboratively with the department chair to oversee a team of 2-3 adjunct piano instructors representing a range of styles and pedagogical approaches. A Doctorate (DMA) in Piano Performance and/or Piano Performance and Pedagogy is required by the time of appointment. The Department of Music encourages students to integrate creative practice with inquiry, preparing them for a lifetime of learning and artistic engagement. We seek candidates with a commitment to and enthusiasm for teaching music majors and non-majors in a vibrant and demanding undergraduate liberal arts context. The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to teach and mentor students with a wide range of backgrounds, abilities, and musical interests. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, teaching statement, creative activity statement including links to video recordings of two recent performances (suggested length: 2 single-spaced pages per statement), a sample of recent recital programs. The teaching statement should detail the candidate’s teaching philosophy and commitment to educating a diverse student body through inclusive pedagogies. The performance videos should include a total of at least five works displaying the candidate’s range. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Department Chair Dr. Andy McGraw (amcgraw@richmond.edu) with any questions. For more information on the Department of Music at the University of Richmond and its programs, please visit music.richmond.edu. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Culinary-Arts-Intern_JR100290": {
      "title": "Culinary Arts Intern",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-05-29\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: The Culinary Arts Intern is an entry level kitchen position. Working under direction, the culinary intern prepares food in quantity, and is responsible for maintaining high standards for food quality while practicing proper sanitation, food safety, and cooking procedures.  **This internship is only for students currently enrolled in a culinary program through an educational institution**  WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT : This position requires long periods of standing and walking, and the ability to lift 50 lbs., in conditions that are often, hot, humid, or cold. Must be able to safely use and operate typical kitchen tools and equipment. Must be able to work outdoors in varying weather conditions, on occasion. QUALIFICATIONS : Basic knowledge of culinary techniques and practices. Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices. Skilled in using effective time management to accomplish tasks. Skilled in reading and written and verbal communication. Ability to understand and follow written and verbal instructions and procedures. Ability to exercise flexibility and to work calmly and efficiently under pressure in a fast-paced environment with frequent interruptions. Ability to provide a high level of customer service. Ability to monitor and maintain quality control standards. Ability to work cooperatively as a part of a team. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE : High school diploma or GED. One year experience preferred. Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire. (This is dependent on the assigned unit.) WORK HOURS: Part-time, Temporary, non-exempt position Schedule may be 4:00 - 8:30 PM weekdays and weekends or 7:00 AM - 4:00 PM; rotating days and weekends; less than 1,000 hrs./year This position is designated as essential personnel and, during inclement weather and emergency situations, is required to be available to the University as needed. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 1 (Rate starting at $15.00 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Tyler-Haynes-Professorship_JR100574": {
      "title": "Tyler Haynes Professorship",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-05-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Jepson School of Leadership Studies (JSLS) at the University of Richmond invites applications for a full professor of leadership studies, with consideration also given to highly qualified advanced associate professors, to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. We seek candidates with a distinguished and ongoing record of scholarship in the study of leadership, and who demonstrate the potential and desire to teach within a vibrant, undergraduate liberal arts context. The successful candidate will have an earned doctorate in a discipline such as organizational behavior, organizational economics, organizational psychology, social psychology, political psychology, public administration, or a related field. Subject to the Board of Trustees’ approval, the successful candidate will be appointed to an endowed professorship in leadership studies with the additional possibility of teaching in the Robins School of Business or the School of Arts and Sciences. The successful candidate will be expected to teach “Theories and Methods of Leadership,” a course required within the Jepson curriculum, and will have the opportunity to develop and teach electives in their areas of specialization. Founded in 1992, the Jepson School of Leadership Studies is the first undergraduate-focused school of leadership studies in the United States. We are a collaborative, inclusive community of active scholars with representation from diverse humanities and social science disciplines. Our curriculum spans the study of political behavior, psychology of social norms, history and literature relevant to social change and leadership, and ethical decision-making. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit a 1) curriculum vitae, 2) cover letter, which demonstrates how their scholarly interests bear on leadership and how they are prepared to contribute to the interdisciplinary, liberal arts community at the Jepson School, and 3) a teaching statement, which describes how they would approach undergraduate teaching (of leadership) within an interdisciplinary liberal arts curriculum. Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence on September 4, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Search Chair Dr. Chris von Rueden ( cvonrued@richmond.edu ) with any questions. For more information on the Jepson School and its programs, please visit http://jepson.richmond.edu/ . The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Custodial-Floor-Technician_JR100325": {
      "title": "Custodial Floor Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-04-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: The Custodial Floor Technician maintains the standards of cleanliness in accordance with operating procedures and adheres to all other University of Richmond policies and guidelines. The Custodial Floor Technician also upholds the Facilities Department’s mission which is to create and maintain an outstanding learning environment through constant attention to our community’s needs and thoughtful care of beautiful campus.  RESPONSIBILITIES: Advocates safety for everyone on campus by identifying, reporting and addressing when appropriate Interacts daily with diverse staff, students and faculty by providing a welcoming and positive atmosphere Preserves the heritage and unique infrastructure of the University by exceeding industry cleanliness standards Cleans assigned areas as directed and follows operating procedures Responsible for the care and upkeep of resources provided to them by the University Operates various types of equipment used for floor care maintenance such as automatic scrubbers, wet/dry vacuums, carpet extractors, burnishers, etc. Performs various tasks including but not limited to mopping, burnishing, stripping, waxing, refinishing, scrubbing and cleaning different floor types. Responds and assists with any custodial related requests as directed WORKING HOURS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Must be able to lift, push and pull up to 50 pounds Must be able to properly operate floor care equipment Must be able to stand for long periods of time, go up and down stairs and walk for long periods Must be able to work outdoors under adverse weather conditions including heat, rain and snow Frequently required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to hazardous materials QUALIFICATIONS: Minimum of a High School degree or educational equivalent preferred One year or more of experience in custodial operations or a related field preferred Professional experience in floor care preferred Ability to effectively communicate with listed contacts above Required: possess a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing WORK HOURS: Full-time, non-exempt position  Monday-Friday 6:00am-2:30pm with some weekend hours required All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are subject to planned overtime work as required by their immediate supervisor. SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 2 (Hiring Range $16.01 to $19.21 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Fitness-Assistant_JR100862": {
      "title": "Fitness Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-04-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description The fitness Assistant monitors the fitness spaces in the Weinstein Center for Recreation and Wellness to ensure cleanliness, safety, and supervision of the facility and its members. Assists with the daily operations of the facility and circulates during shift, maintaining a visible presence in the facility, with a focus on monitoring the upstairs and downstairs fitness spaces. Assist members with questions on how to use equipment properly. Provide safety assistance in the weight room (spotting) as needed. Perform preventative maintenance of fitness equipment (ensure members re-rack weights, clean equipment, organize multipurpose rooms and stretching spaces, etc.). Accurately explain policies & procedures related to group exercise, personal training, and fitness special events. Ensure all facility supplies are maintained (paper towels, hand sanitizer, sanitizing wipes, cleaning spray bottles). Work with the On-duty Facility Supervisor to access supplies. Enforce policies and procedures, and provide customer service by answering any questions members may have while on the floor. Assist with any setup/breakdown or special projects as assigned Collect towels from all towel bins, locker rooms, and sheets from the massage room Wash, dry, and fold towels to maintain inventory on racks Report maintenance and other repair needs to the Equipment Manager, Fitness Manager, and/or On-duty Facility Manager Serve as a first responder in the event of an emergency in the Weinstein Center. Attend and participate in departmental employee training programs. CONTACTS : Weinstein Center Members (students, faculty/staff and community) – to assess satisfaction and communicate essential information. QUALIFICATIONS : Knowledge, skills & ability : Current CPR/AED certification required within 30 days of hire Ability to read, interpret, and enforce policies and procedures Education & experience : Some High School Previous experience in a recreation facility preferred Schedule varies. Some evenings and weekends Hourly Rate : $15.00"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Police-Officer--Virginia-Certified-Officers-and-Non-certified-Applicants-Welcome-_JR100061": {
      "title": "Police Officer (Virginia Certified Officers and Non-certified Applicants Welcome)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-04-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description The University of Richmond Police Department is currently recruiting for a full-time Police Officer (Virginia Certified Officers and Non-certified Applicants Welcome). A sworn law enforcement position to provide law enforcement and crime prevention duties to include CLERY requirements for the University of Richmond within the University of Richmond Police Department.  Applicants who are currently Virginia certified through DCJS will be required to take a Physical Agility Test. You will be notified of date/time and location of the testing.  Non-certified applicants will be required to take both a written and physical agility test. RESPONSIBILITIES: Patrol/Crime Prevention/Community Policing Protects community and property of the University. Patrols campus. Responds to calls of service. Provides safety and security services to the University Community. Promotes a positive community relation in the performance of job duties. Enforces University rules and regulations.  Provides non-police related services as needed.  Willing to work overtime assignments when needed.  The person in this position has been identified as a Campus Security Authority and Responsible Employee for Clery and Title IX purposes respectively, the employee will maintain current training and comply with the responsibilities of both titles. Enforcement Enforces VA Criminal and Traffic Laws.  Makes arrests. Testifies in court. Enforces parking violations. Provides mutual aid and assistance to other law enforcement agencies as requested.  Investigations Conducts criminal investigations, if assigned. Collects and maintain evidence. Writes reports in a clear manner. Initiates administrative reporting process for student violations of university rules and regulations. Training Attends department training to maintain required police standards.  WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Police officers possess a level of fitness and general health to perform physical tasks encountered on the job. These activities may include, but are not limited to: climbing obstacles, running after suspects, running up stairs, jumping over obstacles, lifting heavy objects or persons, subduing persons resisting arrest, and physically pushing movable objects. Officers are physically able to perform the duties and functions of a sworn police officer, carry and use protective equipment, and perform in all weather conditions. Responds, if requested or needed, on weekends and/or nights as a function of this position.  QUALIFICATIONS: Able to operate a motor vehicle.  Maintains all training standards and firearm requirements established by DCJS and URPD.  Able to present to a wide variety of audiences regarding safety issues, and crime prevention topics. Demonstrates computer efficiency in Microsoft office and possesses effective communication skills.  Required: Must have a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: High School Diploma No experience required Virginia Certified Law Enforcement Officer preferred Experience could change depending on current need of the department WORK HOURS: Full-time, non-exempt position 12-hour shift, during a 24-hour day, 40 hours weekly, 7 day period SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 5 (Starting pay as of 7/1/26 is $30.29/hr.)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/UR-Main-Campus/Custodial-Floor-Technician_JR101178": {
      "title": "Custodial Floor Technician",
